const TOPICS = [
  // ─────────────────── MONOPOLY ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m15", "lld_m15_t7", 7, "Design Monopoly", "design-monopoly",
    ["case-study", "polymorphism", "state-machine", "transactions"],
    "A player rolls doubles twice, lands on an unowned Boardwalk, declines to buy — so it goes to auction with four bidders — then on the third doubles goes straight to jail. Two turns later they land on a hotel they cannot pay for and must mortgage, sell houses, and finally go bankrupt, handing every deed to the creditor. How much of your design is a switch statement? Design Monopoly.",
    "The board is not forty cases in a switch — it is forty SQUARE objects, each implementing landedOn(player, ctx), so adding Free Parking house rules touches one class instead of one god method. Rent is DATA (a rent table plus monopoly and building state), not code. And the genuinely hard parts are not the board at all: the turn is a STATE MACHINE with doubles and jail exits, auctions and trades are MULTI-PARTY TRANSACTIONS that must commit atomically or not at all, and bankruptcy is a cascade whose assets must land on the correct creditor — the bank or the player who bankrupted you.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: two to eight players move around a ring of forty squares by rolling two dice, collecting a salary when passing GO. Squares are heterogeneous: PROPERTIES (streets grouped in colour sets, railroads, utilities) that may be bought, developed, mortgaged and charge rent; TAX squares that charge a fixed or percentage amount; CHANCE and COMMUNITY CHEST that draw a card and execute its effect (move, pay, collect, get-out-of-jail-free); GO TO JAIL; JAIL/just-visiting; and FREE PARKING. A player landing on an unowned property may buy it at list price; if they decline, the property goes to AUCTION among all solvent players, starting at any bid. Rent depends on state: base rent, doubled when the owner holds the complete colour set undeveloped, and a table lookup once houses or a hotel are built; railroads scale with how many the owner holds; utilities multiply the dice roll. Owners may build houses evenly across a monopoly (no property may be more than one house ahead of its siblings), sell buildings back at half price, and MORTGAGE undeveloped properties for cash — mortgaged properties charge no rent. Players may TRADE arbitrary bundles of cash, deeds and jail cards. A player who cannot pay must liquidate; if liquidation is insufficient they are BANKRUPT and leave the game, surrendering everything to their creditor. Rolling doubles grants another turn; three consecutive doubles sends the player to jail. Jail is escaped by paying a fine, playing a card, or rolling doubles within three turns. Non-functional constraints that shape the design: the RULE SET is famously variant-heavy (house rules, national editions), so square behaviour and rent economics must be extensible without editing a central dispatcher; every money movement must be conserved (no cash created or destroyed by a partial failure); and the game must be deterministically replayable from a seed and a move log for testing, which pushes randomness behind an injectable Dice interface. Scope: single process, one table, human or scripted players behind one interface."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "GAME owns the turn loop and the game-level state machine: whose turn it is, the doubles counter, the phase within a turn, and the ordered list of active players. BOARD is the ring: an indexed list of forty Squares plus the arithmetic that moves a token (position = (position + roll) % 40) and detects passing GO. SQUARE is the abstract type carrying the polymorphic contract landedOn(Player p, GameContext ctx) — every rule about 'what happens here' lives in a subclass, and this single decision is the difference between a maintainable design and a 600-line switch. PROPERTYSQUARE (with RailroadSquare and UtilitySquare as siblings or strategies) holds owner, mortgaged flag, houseCount, and its RentPolicy; it knows how to compute rent for a given landing context. COLOURGROUP is a first-class entity, not a string on the property: it owns the membership list, answers isMonopoly(owner), and enforces the EVEN BUILD rule across its members — putting that invariant in the group is what stops it being re-implemented in three places. PLAYER holds cash, position, the set of owned deeds, jail state (inJail, jailTurns, getOutCards) and a reference to their decision-maker. BANK is the money authority: it owns the house/hotel inventory (limited in the real game — 32 houses, 12 hotels — a scarcity rule worth mentioning) and exposes transfer(from, to, amount) as the ONLY way money moves, so conservation is enforceable in one place. DECK models Chance and Community Chest as a shuffled queue of CARD objects, each a small command with an execute(player, ctx). AUCTIONSERVICE and TRADESERVICE run the multi-party flows. PLAYERAGENT is the decision interface (buyOrAuction, bid, acceptTrade, whatToLiquidate) that keeps human and bot players interchangeable and makes the whole game simulatable in tests."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "Three patterns earn their place and you should justify each rather than name-drop. POLYMORPHIC DISPATCH over Square is the spine: board.squareAt(pos).landedOn(player, ctx) replaces a switch on a square-type enum. The payoff is Open/Closed in the dimension this game actually grows — new square kinds and house rules — and it also localizes each rule's tests. The anti-pattern the interviewer is fishing for is a resolveLanding(Square s) method with fifteen if-else branches, because that method becomes the file every feature edits and every merge conflicts on. STRATEGY for rent: RentPolicy is separate from PropertySquare because streets, railroads and utilities compute rent by completely different laws (table lookup by houses; count of railroads owned; dice roll multiplier) while sharing everything else about being ownable. Making rent a strategy also lets a house-rule edition swap the economics without touching board structure. COMMAND for Chance and Community Chest cards: each card is an object with an execute, so the deck is data, cards are individually testable, and 'advance to nearest railroad and pay double rent' — the nastiest card in the deck because it re-enters landing resolution — is expressible without special-casing. STATE (as a compact enum plus guarded transitions, not a class hierarchy) for the turn phase and for jail; three or four states do not justify a class per state, and saying so is judgment signal. Two structural choices are deliberate: GameContext is passed into landedOn so squares never reach into globals — they get the bank, the board, the current player list and the dice through one seam, which makes every square unit-testable with a fake context; and PlayerAgent is an interface because the alternative (Game asking a UI directly) makes the game unplayable in tests and un-simulatable for balance work. What is deliberately absent: a rules engine or scripting layer — Monopoly's variability is bounded enough that subclasses plus data tables cover it, and reaching for a DSL here is over-engineering you should be prepared to defend against."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "abstract class Square {\n  final int index; final String name;\n  Square(int i, String n) { index = i; name = n; }\n  // The polymorphic contract that replaces the switch.\n  abstract void landedOn(Player p, GameContext ctx);\n}\n\nclass PropertySquare extends Square {\n  private final ColourGroup group; private final RentPolicy rent;\n  private final int price;\n  Player owner; boolean mortgaged; int houses;      // 0..4, 5 == hotel\n\n  PropertySquare(int i, String n, int price, ColourGroup g, RentPolicy r) {\n    super(i, n); this.price = price; this.group = g; this.rent = r;\n    g.add(this);\n  }\n\n  @Override void landedOn(Player p, GameContext ctx) {\n    if (owner == null) { ctx.offerPurchaseOrAuction(this, p); return; }\n    if (owner == p || mortgaged) return;            // own it / mortgaged: no rent\n    long due = rent.rentFor(this, ctx.lastRoll());\n    ctx.bank().settle(p, owner, due);               // may trigger liquidation\n  }\n\n  boolean canBuildHouse() {                         // EVEN BUILD invariant\n    if (mortgaged || houses >= 5) return false;\n    if (!group.isMonopoly(owner) || group.anyMortgaged()) return false;\n    return houses <= group.minHouses();             // never >1 ahead of siblings\n  }\n  int price() { return price; }\n  ColourGroup group() { return group; }\n}\n\nclass GoToJailSquare extends Square {\n  GoToJailSquare(int i) { super(i, \"Go To Jail\"); }\n  @Override void landedOn(Player p, GameContext ctx) { ctx.sendToJail(p); }\n}\n\nclass Bank {\n  // The ONLY money mover: conservation is enforceable in one place.\n  void transfer(Player from, Player to, long amt) {\n    if (from.cash() < amt) throw new InsufficientFunds(amt - from.cash());\n    from.debit(amt);\n    if (to != null) to.credit(amt);                 // null == the bank itself\n  }\n\n  // Settle a debt, forcing liquidation, then bankruptcy if still short.\n  void settle(Player debtor, Player creditor, long amt) {\n    if (debtor.cash() < amt) debtor.agent().raiseCash(amt - debtor.cash());\n    if (debtor.cash() < amt) { declareBankrupt(debtor, creditor); return; }\n    transfer(debtor, creditor, amt);\n  }\n\n  void declareBankrupt(Player debtor, Player creditor) {\n    for (PropertySquare d : List.copyOf(debtor.deeds())) {\n      d.owner = creditor;                           // to the CREDITOR, not the bank\n      if (creditor == null) { d.owner = null; d.mortgaged = false; }  // bank: re-auction\n      d.houses = 0;                                 // buildings return to inventory\n    }\n    if (creditor != null) creditor.credit(debtor.cash());\n    debtor.setBankrupt();\n  }\n}\n\nclass Game {\n  private int doubles = 0;\n\n  void playTurn(Player p) {\n    doubles = 0;\n    do {\n      Roll r = dice.roll();\n      if (p.inJail() && !resolveJail(p, r)) return;  // still in jail: turn ends\n      if (r.isDouble() && ++doubles == 3) { sendToJail(p); return; }\n      board.advance(p, r.total());                   // handles passing GO\n      board.squareAt(p.position()).landedOn(p, context());\n      if (p.isBankrupt()) return;\n      p.agent().manageAssets(context());             // build / mortgage / trade\n    } while (lastRollWasDouble() && !p.inJail());     // doubles: roll again\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the board as polymorphic squares, and rent as data",
        "Start where every naive Monopoly implementation starts and show why it collapses. Version one has an enum SquareType { PROPERTY, TAX, CHANCE, JAIL, GO, ... } and a method resolveLanding(Player p, Square s) with a switch on s.type. It works on day one. By day ten the switch has branches for railroads and utilities (which are properties but compute rent differently), for the Chance card that moves you to another square and must RE-ENTER landing resolution, and for the house rule that pays Free Parking a pot. Every branch reaches into different fields on a Square struct that is now a union of every square's needs — owner and houses on a struct that also represents GO — and the type field must be checked before touching any of them. That is the classic anemic-data-plus-god-method shape, and its cost is concrete: adding a square kind edits shared code (merge conflicts, regression risk in unrelated rules), the tests must construct a whole game to exercise one branch, and nothing stops a caller reading .houses off a TaxSquare. The correct primitive is POLYMORPHIC DISPATCH: Square is abstract with one abstract method, landedOn(player, ctx), and each kind is a subclass owning exactly its own state and rule. board.squareAt(pos).landedOn(p, ctx) is the entire dispatcher — one line, no branches, and the compiler guarantees every kind implements the contract. The Chance card that moves you becomes trivially correct because ctx.moveTo(square) simply calls landedOn again — recursion through the same contract instead of a special case in the switch. RENT is the second half of the same argument. Candidates commonly write a rentFor method with if (houses == 0 && monopoly) return base*2; else if (houses == 1) ... — arithmetic hard-coded per property tier. Real Monopoly rent is a TABLE printed on the deed: six numbers (base, 1–4 houses, hotel). Model it as data on the deed and rent becomes rentTable[houses], with two rules layered on top: undeveloped-with-monopoly doubles the base, and mortgaged charges nothing. Railroads and utilities do not fit that table at all, which is exactly why rent belongs behind a RentPolicy strategy rather than inside PropertySquare — railroads read the owner's railroad count, utilities multiply the dice roll, and neither is a special case of the street table. When the interviewer asks 'now add a national edition where utilities work differently,' the answer is one new RentPolicy and zero edits elsewhere, which is the whole point."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the turn state machine, auctions and trades as atomic transactions",
        "THE TURN IS A STATE MACHINE and modelling it as straight-line code is where most implementations get subtly wrong rules. The states within a turn are roughly: ROLLING, MOVED/RESOLVING_SQUARE, MANAGING_ASSETS (build, mortgage, trade), and TURN_END — with two loops layered on. Loop one: rolling DOUBLES grants another full roll-and-move, but the THIRD consecutive double sends the player to jail INSTEAD of moving, and the doubles counter must reset per turn, not per game. Candidates routinely implement 'doubles means roll again' as a while loop and forget that going to jail on the third breaks out without moving, or they check the doubles count after moving so the player collects GO money on the way to jail. Loop two: JAIL is its own sub-state machine with three exits — pay the fine immediately, play a get-out-of-jail-free card, or attempt a double on each of up to three turns; on the third failed attempt the player must pay and move by that roll. Critically, a double rolled to LEAVE jail does not grant another turn — a rule that a naive 'if double, repeat' loop gets wrong every time. Write the jail exit as an explicit resolveJail(player, roll) returning whether the player may move, and the rule lives in one auditable place. AUCTIONS AND TRADES are the transactional heart. An auction has multiple bidders, a rising price, withdrawal, and one winner who must actually be able to pay; a trade is an arbitrary bundle — cash, deeds, jail cards — moving in both directions between two players. Both are MULTI-PARTY TRANSACTIONS and both must be ALL-OR-NOTHING. The bug shape is the same in each: mutate as you go. A trade implemented as 'transfer A's cash to B; transfer B's deeds to A; transfer A's deeds to B' can fail on the third step — a deed is mortgaged and the receiver cannot pay the transfer fee, or a validation rejects a property with houses on it (you may not trade developed property; buildings must be sold first) — leaving a half-executed trade: cash moved, deeds did not. The game state is now corrupt in a way no undo path exists for. The fix is the standard two-phase shape: VALIDATE the entire proposal against a snapshot (both parties solvent, every deed actually owned by its stated owner, no developed properties in the bundle, no mortgaged-deed fee unpayable), then APPLY every leg with no possibility of failure, ideally through a single applyTransfers(List<Leg>) that either runs the whole list or throws before touching anything. Auctions get the same treatment: bidding is a pure protocol over an immutable state (current high bid, active bidders) and only the final award — debit winner, assign deed — mutates, and it re-validates solvency because a bidder's cash could have changed if the auction allowed liquidation mid-flow. Say the words 'validate then apply' and 'no partial mutation' — that is the transactional instinct being probed, and it is the same instinct that separates a senior from someone who has only written happy-path game code."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "SUBCLASS-PER-SQUARE vs COMPONENT/BEHAVIOUR OBJECTS: subclassing is the right default here — square kinds are few, stable, and genuinely differ in behaviour — but note the seam where it strains: a house rule that makes Free Parking pay a pot, or an edition where a tax square also awards a card, wants composition (a Square holding a list of LandingEffects) rather than a new subclass per combination. Name the inheritance-explosion risk and the composition escape hatch rather than pretending subclassing is free. RENT AS TABLE DATA vs COMPUTED: tables match the physical deed, are trivially editable for balance work, and serialize cleanly to a JSON board definition (which is how you support multiple editions without code); computed rent is compact but bakes economics into code. Prefer data, and note that a data-driven board file also gives you deterministic test fixtures. IMMUTABLE GAME STATE vs MUTABLE OBJECTS: a functional design where each move returns a new GameState makes undo, replay and AI search (minimax over trades? Monte Carlo over strategies?) nearly free, at the cost of copying and much more allocation; the mutable object graph is more natural to write and faster, but undo requires either a command log with inverse operations or full snapshots. If the interviewer mentions bots or replay, immutability or a command log stops being optional. SYNCHRONOUS vs EVENT-DRIVEN player decisions: the code above calls agent().buyOrAuction() synchronously, which is clean for bots and terrible for a networked UI where a human may take thirty seconds or disconnect; the network version needs the turn state machine to PERSIST across a pause, with a decision timeout that applies a default action — and noticing that the state machine you already built is exactly what makes this possible is the strongest close available. Extension points to name: house rules as a RuleSet object injected at construction (starting cash, GO salary, Free Parking pot, auction on/off); BOT PLAYERS behind PlayerAgent, which the design already supports; NETWORK PLAY, where the atomicity work pays off twice because half-applied trades across a network are unrecoverable; and BUILDING SCARCITY (32 houses, 12 hotels) which turns building into a contended resource and, in a multiplayer server, into the same atomic-decrement problem as seat capacity in a booking system."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Where does the logic for landing on a property live?' — in PropertySquare.landedOn, not in a switch; the board's dispatcher is one polymorphic call and adding a square kind adds a class. 'A Chance card says advance to the nearest railroad and pay double rent — how?' — the card is a Command that moves the token and calls landedOn again with a rent multiplier in the context; because landing resolution is a contract rather than a switch branch, re-entering it is natural instead of a special case. 'How do you stop a player building five houses on one property of a monopoly while the others have none?' — the even-build invariant belongs on ColourGroup (houses <= group.minHouses()), so it cannot be bypassed by any caller; putting it in the UI is the classic mistake. 'A trade fails halfway through — what state is the game in?' — it must be impossible: validate the entire bundle against a snapshot, then apply all legs unconditionally; a trade is a transaction, not a sequence of transfers. 'Player cannot pay rent — walk me through it.' — settle() asks the agent to raise cash (sell buildings at half price, mortgage undeveloped deeds); if still short, bankruptcy transfers ALL assets to the CREDITOR, not the bank — that distinction matters because the creditor inherits a monopoly and the game swings on it; if the creditor is the bank, the properties are returned unowned and re-auctioned. 'Three doubles?' — jail immediately, without moving and without collecting GO; and a double rolled to escape jail does not grant an extra turn. 'How would you test this?' — inject Dice and PlayerAgent; a seeded dice sequence plus scripted agents makes any scenario a deterministic unit test, which is why those two seams exist. 'Make it networked / multiplayer.' — the turn state machine becomes persisted server state with decision timeouts and default actions, money moves stay behind Bank.transfer for conservation and auditing, and the atomic trade/auction work carries over unchanged — the LLD-to-distributed-systems seam."),
    ],
    "Monopoly is the interviewer's favourite trap for the switch-statement instinct: the board is heterogeneous by design, so a candidate who models squares as a struct with a type enum writes a god method within five minutes and spends the rest of the interview defending it. The strong answer makes Square abstract with a landedOn contract, pushes rent behind a strategy because railroads and utilities obey different laws, and puts the even-build rule on the colour group where it cannot be bypassed. Then the real probing starts — the turn state machine with doubles-and-jail (where a straight-line loop gets the rules wrong), auctions and trades as multi-party transactions that must validate-then-apply, and bankruptcy cascading assets to the correct creditor. Candidates who say 'validate the whole trade against a snapshot, then apply every leg' read as having shipped transactional code; candidates who mutate as they go get walked into a half-applied trade and have no answer.",
    [
      G("switch_on_square_type", "Modelling squares as one struct with a type enum and resolving landings in a central switch or if-else ladder.", "Make Square abstract with landedOn(player, ctx); each kind is a subclass owning its own state and rule, and the dispatcher becomes one polymorphic call — new square kinds and house rules add classes instead of editing shared code.", "enum SquareType plus a resolveLanding switch; fields like owner/houses on a struct that also represents GO and Free Parking; Chance 'move to' handled as an extra branch rather than re-entering landing resolution."),
      G("nonatomic_trade", "Executing a trade or auction award as a sequence of transfers that mutate as they go, leaving half-applied state when a later leg fails validation or funds.", "Two-phase it: validate the entire proposal against a snapshot (solvency, ownership, no developed properties, mortgage fees payable), then apply every leg through one applyTransfers call that cannot fail midway.", "transfer() calls interleaved with validation inside a trade loop; no rollback path; game states where cash moved but deeds did not after a rejected leg."),
      G("turn_loop_rule_drift", "Writing the turn as a straight-line while-loop on doubles — so the third double still moves the player, GO is collected on the way to jail, or a double rolled to escape jail grants another turn.", "Model the turn explicitly: a per-turn doubles counter checked BEFORE moving (third double goes to jail without moving), and a separate resolveJail(player, roll) whose success permits movement but never grants an extra roll.", "doubles counter incremented after board.advance; no jail sub-state machine; jail escape handled inside the same 'if double, repeat' loop as normal doubles."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.monopoly, "Monopoly class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── 2048 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m15", "lld_m15_t8", 8, "Design the 2048 Game", "design-2048",
    ["case-study", "algorithm", "grid", "state-machine"],
    "A row reads 2, 2, 4 and the player swipes LEFT. Is the result 4, 4 or 8? Now swipe a row of 4, 4, 8, 8 — how many merges happen, and can the 8 produced by the first merge immediately merge again? Then answer the two questions every 2048 implementation gets wrong: when exactly does a new tile spawn, and when exactly is the game over? Design 2048.",
    "The whole game is ONE routine — slide-and-merge a single row to the left — plus a grid rotation. Four directions do not need four near-duplicate methods; rotate the grid so the move becomes 'left', run the one routine on each row, rotate back. The subtlety that separates correct from almost-correct is the merge contract: a tile merges AT MOST ONCE per move, and merges resolve in the direction of travel, so 2,2,4 left is 4,4 and never 8. Everything else follows: a move is only a move if something CHANGED (spawn only then), and game over is no empty cell AND no adjacent equal pair — not merely a full grid.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a 4x4 grid (size configurable) of tiles holding powers of two; empty cells are modelled as 0. The game starts with two random tiles. On each move the player picks one of four DIRECTIONS; every tile slides as far as it can in that direction, and two tiles of equal value that collide merge into one tile of double the value, adding that value to the score. After a move that CHANGED the grid, exactly one new tile spawns in a uniformly random empty cell, with value 2 at 90% probability and 4 at 10%. A move that changes nothing is not a move: no spawn, no score, no turn consumed. The player WINS when a 2048 tile appears (play may optionally continue), and LOSES when no legal move remains. The system must expose: move(direction) returning whether anything changed, the current grid and score, a game-state query (PLAYING, WON, LOST), restart, and typically UNDO of the last move. Non-functional constraints that shape the design: the merge rules must be exactly right because players notice instantly and the bugs are subtle rather than crashing; the same code must serve a UI, a test harness and an AI (expectimax search over moves is a standard extension), so move application must be PURE with respect to randomness — the spawn is the only random step and it belongs behind an injectable RNG so that a seeded game is deterministically replayable and an AI can simulate a move without spawning; and the implementation must not duplicate the merge logic per direction, because four copies of a subtle algorithm means four places for the same bug to hide and three of them will drift. Scope: single player, in-memory, no persistence beyond an optional serialized grid."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "The object census is small and that is the honest answer — this problem is an ALGORITHM design wearing an LLD costume, and the interviewer is grading whether you notice which part carries the difficulty. GRID owns the int[size][size] board with 0 for empty, plus the primitive queries everything else is built on: emptyCells(), get/set, and equality against a snapshot. It should also own the geometric TRANSFORMS — transpose() and reverseRows() (or rotate90) — because those are grid operations, not game rules, and keeping them here is what lets the game layer stay tiny. SLIDEENGINE (or a static slideRowLeft(int[] row) if you prefer a function) is the single load-bearing routine: compress out the zeros, merge equal ADJACENT pairs scanning from the target edge with a merged-this-move guard, compress again, and report the score gained. GAME2048 is the orchestrator: it maps a Direction to a transform, applies the engine row by row, un-transforms, compares against the pre-move snapshot to decide whether anything changed, spawns on change, updates the score, and recomputes the game state. SPAWNPOLICY encapsulates 'uniform random empty cell, 90/10 value split' behind an injectable RNG — a separate object precisely so tests can force a deterministic sequence and an AI can simulate moves WITHOUT spawning. MOVERESULT is a small value object (changed, scoreDelta, optionally the list of merges for animation) so the caller need not diff the grid itself. UNDOSTACK holds prior (grid, score) snapshots — for a 4x4 grid of ints, a full snapshot is 64 bytes and copying it is cheaper than reasoning about inverse operations, which is the right call and worth saying out loud. GAMESTATE is an enum: PLAYING, WON, LOST."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The single most important design decision is DIRECTION NORMALIZATION, and it is worth stating as a principle before any code: implement ONE direction, reduce the other three to it. The naive design writes moveLeft, moveRight, moveUp and moveDown as four methods, each iterating the grid with different index arithmetic. All four contain the same twenty-line merge algorithm with the loop bounds and step direction permuted. That is not four features — it is one algorithm copied four times, and the copies WILL diverge: the classic production symptom is that merging works correctly left and up but double-merges right and down, because the reversed scan direction was transcribed with an off-by-one. The correct structure is: canonicalize the grid so the requested direction becomes LEFT (LEFT = identity; RIGHT = reverse each row; UP = transpose; DOWN = transpose then reverse each row), run slideRowLeft on every row, then apply the INVERSE transform to restore orientation. Now the merge algorithm exists exactly once, is unit-testable as a pure function on an int array (feed it [2,2,4] and assert [4,4,0]), and adding a hypothetical hexagonal or 5x5 variant changes only the transform table. This is the Template-Method idea reduced to its useful core without ceremony — the varying part is the coordinate transform, the invariant part is the merge. Second decision: PURITY of the move. move() must compute the new grid from the old one and only then decide to spawn, because the spawn depends on whether anything changed; interleaving spawn into the slide makes the change detection impossible and the AI simulation useless. Third: change detection by SNAPSHOT COMPARISON (Arrays.deepEquals against the pre-move copy) rather than by threading a boolean through the merge code — the boolean approach is where 'a move that only merged but did not translate any tile' gets mis-reported as unchanged, silently freezing the game. Copying a 4x4 int grid to detect change is free; getting the flag right in four code paths is not. What is deliberately absent: a Tile class with identity and animation state. For core game logic tiles are values, not entities; a UI that wants slide animations needs tile identity, and the clean way to add it is to have the engine emit a list of MOVES and MERGES (from-cell, to-cell, resulting value) alongside the new grid rather than to make the model carry view concerns."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "enum Direction { LEFT, RIGHT, UP, DOWN }\n\nclass Game2048 {\n  private final int n;\n  private int[][] g;\n  private long score;\n  private final Random rng;\n  private final Deque<int[][]> undo = new ArrayDeque<>();\n\n  Game2048(int n, Random rng) {\n    this.n = n; this.rng = rng; this.g = new int[n][n];\n    spawn(); spawn();\n  }\n\n  /** THE single merge routine: slide one row toward index 0. */\n  static long slideRowLeft(int[] row) {\n    int n = row.length;\n    int[] out = new int[n];\n    int w = 0;                       // write cursor\n    long gained = 0;\n    int pending = 0;                 // last value written, eligible to merge once\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n      int v = row[r];\n      if (v == 0) continue;          // compress: skip holes\n      if (pending == v) {            // merge with the tile just written\n        out[w - 1] = v * 2;\n        gained += v * 2;\n        pending = 0;                 // CRITICAL: the merged tile cannot merge again\n      } else {\n        out[w++] = v;\n        pending = v;\n      }\n    }\n    System.arraycopy(out, 0, row, 0, n);\n    return gained;\n  }\n\n  public MoveResult move(Direction d) {\n    int[][] before = copy(g);\n    g = toLeft(g, d);                // normalize: make the move a LEFT move\n    long gained = 0;\n    for (int[] row : g) gained += slideRowLeft(row);\n    g = fromLeft(g, d);              // restore orientation\n\n    boolean changed = !Arrays.deepEquals(before, g);\n    if (!changed) return MoveResult.noop();   // no spawn, no score, no turn\n\n    undo.push(before);\n    score += gained;\n    spawn();\n    return new MoveResult(true, gained, state());\n  }\n\n  private int[][] toLeft(int[][] m, Direction d) {\n    switch (d) {\n      case LEFT:  return m;\n      case RIGHT: return reverseRows(m);\n      case UP:    return transpose(m);\n      case DOWN:  return reverseRows(transpose(m));\n      default:    throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n    }\n  }\n  private int[][] fromLeft(int[][] m, Direction d) {\n    switch (d) {                     // exact inverse of toLeft\n      case LEFT:  return m;\n      case RIGHT: return reverseRows(m);\n      case UP:    return transpose(m);\n      case DOWN:  return transpose(reverseRows(m));\n      default:    throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n    }\n  }\n\n  private void spawn() {\n    List<int[]> empty = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++)\n      for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) if (g[r][c] == 0) empty.add(new int[]{r, c});\n    if (empty.isEmpty()) return;\n    int[] cell = empty.get(rng.nextInt(empty.size()));\n    g[cell[0]][cell[1]] = rng.nextInt(10) == 0 ? 4 : 2;    // 90% 2, 10% 4\n  }\n\n  public GameState state() {\n    boolean won = false;\n    for (int[] row : g) for (int v : row) if (v >= 2048) won = true;\n    if (won) return GameState.WON;\n    return movesRemain() ? GameState.PLAYING : GameState.LOST;\n  }\n\n  /** Game over = no empty cell AND no adjacent equal pair. */\n  private boolean movesRemain() {\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++)\n      for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n        if (g[r][c] == 0) return true;\n        if (c + 1 < n && g[r][c] == g[r][c + 1]) return true;\n        if (r + 1 < n && g[r][c] == g[r + 1][c]) return true;\n      }\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  public boolean undo() {\n    if (undo.isEmpty()) return false;\n    g = undo.pop();\n    return true;\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: one slide routine plus rotation — and the exact merge semantics",
        "Two things carry this problem and both live in twenty lines. FIRST, DIRECTION REDUCTION. Write moveLeft/moveRight/moveUp/moveDown separately and you have written the merge algorithm four times with permuted index arithmetic; the duplication is not a style complaint, it is where the bugs live, because the reversed-direction copies are transcribed by hand and the off-by-one in the merge scan only manifests in one of the four. Reduce instead: LEFT is the canonical move, RIGHT is 'reverse each row, slide left, reverse back', UP is 'transpose, slide left, transpose back', DOWN is 'transpose, reverse rows, slide left, then reverse rows and transpose back' — note that the inverse of a composition applies the steps in REVERSE order, which is the one place candidates slip (composing transpose-then-reverse and undoing with transpose-then-reverse works for the self-inverse pieces individually but not for the composition, so write fromLeft as the deliberate mirror of toLeft and test all four directions round-trip on a random grid). The transforms are O(n^2) and n is 4; worrying about their cost is premature by a factor of a million, whereas the correctness payoff is total: the merge exists ONCE and is a pure function you can exhaustively unit test. SECOND, THE MERGE CONTRACT, which is where interviewers actually score you. Rule one: a tile that has just been PRODUCED by a merge may not merge again in the same move. Row 4,4,8,8 slid left is 8,16 — the 8 produced by merging the two 4s does not then merge with the 8 that follows, even though they are now adjacent and equal. Implementations that compress-then-loop-and-merge-adjacent-pairs-repeatedly produce 32 and are wrong. The guard in the code is the pending variable being zeroed after a merge — a one-line encoding of 'this output slot is closed'. Rule two: merges resolve IN THE DIRECTION OF TRAVEL, which decides the 2,2,4 question. Sliding left, the leftmost eligible pair merges first: 2,2 become 4, and the pre-existing 4 lands beside it — result 4,4,0,0. It is NOT 8: the merged 4 is a fresh tile and rule one closes it. Slide the same row RIGHT and you get 0,0,4,4 — the same pair merges, because the 2,2 are still the only equal adjacent pair — but on a row like 4,2,2 the direction matters visibly: left gives 4,4 and right gives 0,4,4 with the merge resolved from the right edge. State this by walking the scan order: iterate from the destination edge inward, and each output slot may absorb at most one merge. Rule three: sliding and merging are ONE pass conceptually but easiest to reason about as compress, merge, compress — and the code above fuses them into a single write-cursor pass, which is the version worth being able to write on a whiteboard because it makes both rules structurally visible rather than commented."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: move validity, game-over detection, spawn policy and undo",
        "MOVE VALIDITY is the rule most implementations get silently wrong, and the symptom is a game that keeps spawning tiles when the player swipes into a wall until the board fills with garbage. A move counts only if the grid actually CHANGED — some tile translated or some pair merged. Pressing LEFT on a row that is already 4,2,0,0 does nothing to that row; if every row is unchanged, no tile spawns, the score does not move, and the player has not used a turn. The robust detection is a snapshot compare: copy the grid before, deepEquals after. Candidates often try to thread a boolean out of the slide routine instead, and it is easy to set it on translation while missing the pure-merge case (2,2,0,0 sliding left becomes 4,0,0,0 — the write cursor moved zero net positions in some formulations) or vice versa, and the resulting bug is intermittent and hard to see. The snapshot costs 64 bytes on a 4x4 grid; take it. GAME OVER is the second classic. 'The grid is full' is NOT game over: 2,4,2,4 / 4,2,4,2 is full and lost, but 2,2,4,8 / ... is full and still playable because the adjacent 2s can merge. The correct predicate is: there exists no empty cell AND there exists no pair of orthogonally adjacent equal tiles. The scan in movesRemain() checks each cell against only its RIGHT and DOWN neighbours, which covers every adjacency exactly once — checking all four directions per cell doubles the work and tests each pair twice. Note also the framing: 'a legal move exists' is exactly 'some direction would change the grid', so an equally valid (and more obviously correct) implementation simulates all four directions on a copy and asks whether any produced a change — slower, but self-evidently right, and it is the version an AI needs anyway for enumerating legal moves. SPAWN POLICY: uniformly random among EMPTY cells only (a spawn must never overwrite a tile), 90% 2 and 10% 4, and — the part people forget — the spawn happens AFTER the slide and only on a changed grid. Two design consequences: the RNG must be injectable so a seeded game replays identically for bug reports and tests, and the spawn must be separable from move application so an AI can evaluate the deterministic slide result before the random tile lands (expectimax needs exactly this split: a max node over the four directions, then a chance node over every empty cell times {2,4} with the 0.9/0.1 weights). UNDO: because a move is not invertible in general — merges destroy information, and the spawn is random — the honest implementation is a SNAPSHOT STACK, not an inverse operation. Push the pre-move grid and score; undo pops. For a 4x4 int grid this is trivially cheap, and the usual policy question ('how many undos?') is answered by bounding the deque. If the interviewer pushes on memory, note that the grid packs into a single long (16 cells x 4 bits, since values are powers of two up to 2^15), which is the representation high-performance 2048 AIs use and which makes an undo entry eight bytes and the whole transposition table feasible."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "ROTATE-AND-REUSE vs FOUR METHODS vs DIRECTION VECTORS: rotation gives one algorithm and pays two O(n^2) transforms per move — the right default. Four hand-written methods are marginally faster and reliably buggier. The middle option is a single generic slide parameterized by a (dr, dc) direction vector and a start-corner, which avoids the copies but makes the merge loop's index arithmetic much harder to read and to test — it is what you reach for if profiling ever showed the transforms mattering, which for a human-played 4x4 it never will (it can matter inside an AI search doing millions of simulated moves, and there the bitboard representation wins anyway). int[][] vs BITBOARD: the 2D array is readable and obvious; packing the grid into one 64-bit long with 4 bits per cell lets you precompute the slide result for every possible 16-bit ROW into a lookup table, turning a whole move into four table lookups — a 100x speedup that only matters for AI search, and mentioning it demonstrates you know where the ceiling is without prematurely reaching for it. MUTABLE vs IMMUTABLE grid: returning a new grid per move makes undo, AI simulation and replay trivial and is a natural fit here given how small the state is; mutating in place with a snapshot stack is what the code above does and is fine. If the interviewer mentions an AI, immutability (or at least a pure applyMove(grid, dir) -> grid function with no spawn) stops being a preference and becomes a requirement, because search must explore moves without disturbing the real game. TILES AS VALUES vs TILES AS ENTITIES: the model treats tiles as ints, which is correct for rules; smooth UI animation needs per-tile identity to know which tile slid where, and the clean seam is to have the slide emit a MoveResult carrying the (from, to, mergedWith) list rather than to give the model an animation-aware Tile class — keep view state out of the rules. EXTENSIONS worth naming: variable board size (already parameterized; only the win threshold is hard-coded and should be config); a different win tile or endless mode (make 2048 a target value, and let WON be a one-time notification rather than a terminal state); an AI player behind the pure move function (expectimax with a corner-weighted heuristic and monotonicity/smoothness terms is the standard); and multiplayer/daily-challenge modes, which need the seeded RNG the design already has so every player gets the identical tile sequence — a nice payoff for having made randomness injectable at the start."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Do you write four move methods?' — no: one slideRowLeft plus transpose/reverse to normalize every direction to LEFT, and the inverse transform applied in reverse order to restore orientation; the merge algorithm must exist exactly once. 'Row 2,2,4 slid left?' — 4,4: the two 2s merge into a fresh 4 which is then CLOSED for the rest of the move, so it cannot absorb the existing 4; anyone answering 8 has not implemented the merge-once rule. 'Row 4,4,8,8 slid left?' — 8,16, for the same reason; a repeated-pass merge would produce 32 and is the classic bug. 'When does a tile spawn?' — only after a move that CHANGED the grid, in a uniformly random empty cell, 90% a 2 and 10% a 4; swiping into a wall spawns nothing and consumes no turn. 'How do you detect that nothing changed?' — snapshot the grid before and deepEquals after; threading a boolean out of the merge misses the merge-without-translation case. 'Is a full grid game over?' — no: game over is no empty cell AND no orthogonally adjacent equal pair; equivalently, no direction would change the grid. Scanning right and down neighbours only covers each adjacency once. 'Implement undo.' — snapshot stack of (grid, score); merges destroy information and the spawn is random, so there is no inverse operation to apply — and a 4x4 grid snapshot is 64 bytes, or 8 if you pack it into a long. 'Add an AI.' — expose a pure applyMove(grid, direction) with no spawn, then run expectimax: max over four directions, chance node over empty cells x {2,4} weighted 0.9/0.1, with a heuristic favouring a monotone board and a big tile in a corner; the bitboard-plus-row-lookup-table representation is what makes the search deep enough to matter. 'Bigger board or different win tile?' — both are already parameters; only the hard-coded 2048 threshold needs lifting into config, which is the extensibility test the question is really asking."),
    ],
    "2048 is the deceptive one: the object model is tiny, so the interviewer is grading algorithm precision and the instinct to eliminate duplication rather than class-diagram fluency. The first signal is direction handling — a candidate who starts writing moveUp after moveLeft has already lost the point, because the answer is one slide routine plus a rotation. The second is the merge contract, and it is probed with exact rows: 2,2,4 sliding left is 4,4 (the merged tile is closed for the move) and 4,4,8,8 is 8,16, not 32. The third and fourth are the rules everyone implements sloppily: a move counts only if the grid changed (so a wall swipe spawns nothing), and game over requires no empty cell AND no adjacent equal pair rather than merely a full board. Strong candidates also make the spawn RNG injectable and keep move application pure of it, which is what makes seeded replay and an expectimax AI possible later — and they say why undo is a snapshot stack rather than an inverse operation.",
    [
      G("four_direction_copies", "Writing moveLeft, moveRight, moveUp and moveDown as four separate methods, each re-implementing the merge with permuted index arithmetic.", "Normalize every direction to LEFT via transpose/reverse, run ONE slideRowLeft over the rows, then apply the exact inverse transform (composition undone in reverse order); the merge algorithm exists once and is unit-testable as a pure function on an int array.", "four near-identical ~20-line methods; bugs that appear only in right/down moves; no pure row-level function that can be tested with [2,2,4] -> [4,4,0]."),
      G("double_merge", "Letting a tile produced by a merge merge again in the same move — repeated compress-and-merge passes turning 4,4,8,8 into 32, or 2,2,4 into 8.", "Enforce merge-once per output slot: after writing a merged value, close that slot (zero the pending value) so the next tile starts a new slot; scan from the destination edge so merges resolve in the direction of travel.", "a while-loop that keeps merging until no adjacent equal pair remains; results of 32 for row 4,4,8,8; no explicit 'already merged' guard in the slide routine."),
      G("spawn_and_gameover_rules", "Spawning a tile after every keypress regardless of whether the grid changed, and declaring game over as soon as the board is full.", "Detect change by snapshot comparison and spawn only on change (no change = no turn, no score); test game over as no empty cell AND no orthogonally adjacent equal pair — equivalently, no direction would change the grid.", "spawn() called unconditionally at the end of move(); isGameOver implemented as emptyCells().isEmpty(); a full-but-playable board (2,2,4,8 rows) reported as LOST."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.g2048, "2048 game class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── IN-PROCESS EVENT BUS ─────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t1", 1, "Design an In-Process Event Bus", "design-event-bus",
    ["case-study", "observer", "concurrency", "decoupling"],
    "An OrderPlaced event fans out to eight handlers. The third one throws, and the remaining five never run. The sixth publishes an InventoryLow event from inside its own handler — does that get delivered before or after the rest of OrderPlaced? Meanwhile a handler unsubscribes itself mid-dispatch and the whole publish blows up with ConcurrentModificationException. And after a week of uptime the heap is full of listeners belonging to objects nobody references any more. Design an in-process Event Bus.",
    "The registry is a Map from event TYPE to a list of handlers, and the first real design decision is that lookup walks UP the type hierarchy so a handler for Event or an interface receives subclasses. The three mechanisms that carry the difficulty are all about the dispatch loop: the handler list must be COPY-ON-WRITE (or iterated over a snapshot) so subscribing or unsubscribing during dispatch cannot corrupt the iteration; every handler invocation must be wrapped in try/catch so one throwing subscriber cannot abort the other seven; and synchronous dispatch makes a nested publish RE-ENTRANT, which turns delivery order into a depth-first surprise. The leak nobody mentions until production: a bus holds strong references to handlers forever.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a process-local publish/subscribe hub. subscribe(Class<T> eventType, Consumer<T> handler) registers interest and returns a SUBSCRIPTION handle that can be cancelled; publish(Object event) delivers the event to every handler registered for that event's type — and, by design decision, for its supertypes and interfaces, so a handler on a base Event type or a marker interface sees everything relevant. Handlers may be registered and cancelled at any time, including from inside a handler. Dispatch may be SYNCHRONOUS (on the publishing thread, delivery complete when publish returns) or ASYNCHRONOUS (handed to an executor, publish returns immediately); the bus should support both, ideally per-subscription. Optional but commonly probed: handler PRIORITY or ordering guarantees, a dead-event hook for events with no subscribers (a genuinely useful debugging feature — silent no-op delivery is how typo'd event types hide), and a sticky/replay mode where a late subscriber immediately receives the last event of its type. Non-functional constraints that ARE the design: THREAD SAFETY — publishes and subscribes race from many threads, and the iteration of a handler list must tolerate concurrent mutation; ERROR ISOLATION — subscribers are independent by contract, so one failing must not prevent or reorder the others, and the failure must be observable rather than swallowed; RE-ENTRANCY — a handler publishing another event must not deadlock or corrupt state; and LIFECYCLE — the bus must not become the reason short-lived objects are never collected. Explicitly out of scope, and worth saying so: cross-process delivery, durability, retries and ordering across restarts — the moment you need those you want a real broker, and the in-process bus should not grow into a bad one."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "EVENTBUS is the facade: subscribe, unsubscribe, publish, and (optionally) a shutdown that drains the async executor. SUBSCRIBERREGISTRY owns the routing table — a ConcurrentHashMap<Class<?>, CopyOnWriteArrayList<Subscription>> — and the two operations that matter: register/deregister, and handlersFor(Class<?> eventType), which is where the type-hierarchy walk lives. That lookup is worth an entity of its own because it is the expensive part: for an event class, you must collect handlers registered against the class itself, every superclass up to Object, and every interface those classes implement (transitively) — a walk that should be COMPUTED ONCE PER EVENT CLASS AND CACHED, because it depends only on the class, never on the instance. SUBSCRIPTION is the handle: the event type, the handler reference, the delivery mode (sync/async), an optional priority, and a cancel() that removes it from the registry — returning a handle rather than requiring callers to pass the same lambda back to unsubscribe is a real API decision, because a lambda is not equal to another structurally identical lambda and 'unsubscribe by lambda' silently fails, a bug that has shipped in many hand-rolled buses. DISPATCHER executes a delivery: synchronous mode invokes inline on the caller's thread; asynchronous mode submits to an ExecutorService. EXCEPTIONHANDLER (or a SubscriberExceptionHandler callback) receives (event, subscription, throwable) so failures are logged, metered and surfaced rather than swallowed — a bus that quietly eats exceptions is the single worst debugging experience in this design space. DEADEVENT is the fallback wrapper published when no handler matched. Optionally a WEAKSUBSCRIPTION variant holding a WeakReference to the handler's owner for the leak problem."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "This is the OBSERVER pattern promoted from per-subject listener lists to a central mediator, and articulating why that promotion is worth it is the framing: with plain Observer, every publisher holds and manages its own listener list, so subjects must know their observers' registration lifecycle and cross-cutting concerns (auditing every domain event, say) require touching every subject. The bus inverts it — publishers know only the event type, subscribers know only the event type, and neither has a reference to the other. That decoupling is the value proposition AND the cost, and a senior answer states both: you have traded compile-time traceability (find-usages on a method call) for runtime wiring, so 'who handles OrderPlaced?' becomes unanswerable from the IDE. That is why a dead-event hook and a way to enumerate registrations are not luxuries. The routing table is a ConcurrentHashMap keyed by Class, with CopyOnWriteArrayList values — and that value type is a deliberate, load-bearing choice, not a reflex: dispatch iterates far more often than subscription mutates, and copy-on-write makes iteration lock-free and snapshot-consistent, which is exactly the property that makes mid-dispatch subscribe/unsubscribe safe. Delivery mode is a per-subscription STRATEGY rather than a bus-wide setting, because the same event legitimately wants a fast inline handler (update an in-memory projection) and a slow async one (send an email); forcing one mode on the whole bus pushes every consumer to the slowest common denominator. Error isolation is structural, not optional: the dispatch loop wraps each invocation in try/catch and routes the throwable to the ExceptionHandler, then continues — the alternative (letting it propagate) makes delivery depend on handler registration order, which is a nondeterministic coupling between unrelated modules. What is deliberately absent: retries, ordering guarantees across event types, and persistence. Each of those is a broker feature; putting them in an in-process bus produces a half-broker with none of the durability guarantees, and the honest design boundary is 'if you need delivery guarantees, this is the wrong component' — say that line, it reads as production experience."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "class EventBus {\n  private final Map<Class<?>, CopyOnWriteArrayList<Subscription>> registry =\n      new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  // Type-hierarchy walk is per-CLASS and immutable: cache it.\n  private final Map<Class<?>, List<Class<?>>> typeCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  private final ExecutorService async;\n  private final BiConsumer<Object, Throwable> onError;\n\n  EventBus(ExecutorService async, BiConsumer<Object, Throwable> onError) {\n    this.async = async; this.onError = onError;\n  }\n\n  public <T> Subscription subscribe(Class<T> type, Consumer<? super T> handler, boolean asyncMode) {\n    Subscription s = new Subscription(type, handler, asyncMode, this);\n    registry.computeIfAbsent(type, k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>()).add(s);\n    return s;                                   // handle, not the lambda, is the key\n  }\n\n  void remove(Subscription s) {\n    CopyOnWriteArrayList<Subscription> list = registry.get(s.type());\n    if (list != null) list.remove(s);           // safe DURING an in-flight dispatch\n  }\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  public void publish(Object event) {\n    boolean delivered = false;\n    for (Class<?> t : typesOf(event.getClass())) {       // self + supers + interfaces\n      CopyOnWriteArrayList<Subscription> list = registry.get(t);\n      if (list == null) continue;\n      for (Subscription s : list) {             // iterates a stable SNAPSHOT\n        delivered = true;\n        deliver(s, event);\n      }\n    }\n    if (!delivered && !(event instanceof DeadEvent)) publish(new DeadEvent(event));\n  }\n\n  private void deliver(Subscription s, Object event) {\n    if (s.async()) {\n      async.execute(() -> invokeIsolated(s, event));\n      return;\n    }\n    invokeIsolated(s, event);                   // SYNC: re-entrant, depth-first\n  }\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  private void invokeIsolated(Subscription s, Object event) {\n    try {\n      ((Consumer<Object>) s.handler()).accept(event);\n    } catch (Throwable t) {                     // ERROR ISOLATION: one bad handler\n      onError.accept(event, t);                 // cannot abort the other seven\n    }\n  }\n\n  private List<Class<?>> typesOf(Class<?> c) {\n    return typeCache.computeIfAbsent(c, k -> {\n      List<Class<?>> all = new ArrayList<>();\n      for (Class<?> x = k; x != null && x != Object.class; x = x.getSuperclass()) {\n        all.add(x);\n        Collections.addAll(all, x.getInterfaces());\n      }\n      return List.copyOf(all);\n    });\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class Subscription implements AutoCloseable {\n  private final Class<?> type; private final Consumer<?> handler;\n  private final boolean async; private final EventBus bus;\n  Subscription(Class<?> t, Consumer<?> h, boolean a, EventBus b) {\n    type = t; handler = h; async = a; bus = b;\n  }\n  Class<?> type() { return type; }\n  Consumer<?> handler() { return handler; }\n  boolean async() { return async; }\n  public void close() { bus.remove(this); }     // try-with-resources / explicit cancel\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: registry lookup up the type hierarchy, and dispatch that survives mutation",
        "TYPE-HIERARCHY LOOKUP. The naive registry is Map<Class, List<Handler>> with an exact-match get, and it fails the first time someone writes subscribe(Event.class, auditLogger) expecting to see every domain event, or subscribes to an interface like AuditableEvent. Exact matching silently delivers nothing — the worst failure mode, because there is no error, just absence. The correct lookup enumerates the event's FULL type set: the concrete class, every superclass up the chain, and every interface implemented at each level (transitively, since interfaces extend interfaces). For OrderPlaced extends DomainEvent implements Auditable, publishing an OrderPlaced must hit handlers registered on OrderPlaced, DomainEvent, and Auditable. Two engineering notes make this a senior answer rather than a textbook one. First, the walk is a pure function of the CLASS, so compute it once and cache it in a ConcurrentHashMap keyed by class — otherwise you are doing reflection-ish traversal on every publish, and publishes vastly outnumber distinct event types. Second, hierarchy delivery makes ORDER ambiguous: does the OrderPlaced handler run before the DomainEvent handler? Pick a rule (most-specific-first is the least surprising), document it, and resist promising more than you can keep — handlers within one type are in registration order, which is itself a weak guarantee callers should not depend on. THE CONCURRENTMODIFICATION TRAP. Consider a handler that unsubscribes itself after the first delivery — an extremely common pattern (one-shot listeners, teardown on shutdown events). With an ArrayList value and a plain for-each, the removal during iteration throws ConcurrentModificationException and aborts the entire dispatch, so handlers after it never run. Guarding the whole dispatch with a lock is worse: the handler's unsubscribe call tries to take the same lock, and either you deadlock (non-reentrant) or you mutate the list you are iterating (reentrant) and throw anyway. The correct primitive is to ITERATE A SNAPSHOT. CopyOnWriteArrayList gives exactly this for free: its iterator is over the array that existed at iteration start, so subscribes and unsubscribes during dispatch are visible to the NEXT publish and invisible to this one — a clear, defensible semantic. The cost is that every subscribe/unsubscribe copies the array, which is fine because the read/write ratio is enormous (thousands of publishes per subscription change); state that ratio as the justification, because copy-on-write chosen without it is cargo-culting. The manual alternative — take the lock, copy the list to a local array, release the lock, then iterate — is worth mentioning as the same idea implemented by hand, and it is what you would write if handler lists were huge and mutation frequent. Either way, the rule is: NEVER hold a lock while invoking a handler, because you have no idea what user code does inside it, and holding a lock across foreign code is how deadlocks get designed in."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: sync vs async ordering, re-entrant publishes, error isolation and the listener leak",
        "SYNCHRONOUS DISPATCH runs handlers on the publisher's thread, so publish() returns only when every handler has finished — simple, debuggable (one stack trace spans publisher and handler), and transactional-friendly (handlers can participate in the caller's transaction). Its consequence is RE-ENTRANCY: a handler that itself publishes creates a nested dispatch, and delivery becomes DEPTH-FIRST. Concretely, publishing A with handlers [H1, H2, H3] where H2 publishes B means the delivery order is H1(A), H2(A), all of B's handlers, then H3(A). Candidates are routinely surprised by this and it matters: an observer that assumed all of A's handlers had run before any B handler saw the world will read half-updated state. Two mitigations, and the choice is a real one. Depth-first is the default and is fine if handlers are independent; if you need breadth-first (finish the current event, then drain nested ones), you queue nested publishes in a ThreadLocal ArrayDeque and drain it after the top-level dispatch completes — which is exactly what Guava's EventBus does with its per-thread queue, and naming that is a good signal. Also guard against unbounded recursion: A triggers B triggers A is an infinite loop that either stack-overflows (sync) or spins forever (queued), so a depth counter or a cycle detector in debug builds earns its place. ASYNCHRONOUS DISPATCH hands each delivery to an executor: publish returns immediately, a slow handler cannot stall the publisher, and one handler's exception is naturally isolated on its own thread. The costs are the ones people forget — you lose ordering between handlers and often between EVENTS (two publishes can be processed out of order), the stack trace no longer includes the publisher (so capture the publishing context and attach it to error reports), backpressure becomes a real question (an unbounded executor queue is a memory leak with extra steps; a bounded queue makes publish() block or drop, which is a policy the caller must know about), and shutdown needs a drain. ERROR ISOLATION is non-negotiable in both modes: subscribers are independent by contract, so the dispatch loop wraps every invocation in try/catch, routes the throwable to an exception handler with the event and subscription attached, and CONTINUES. Without it, delivery depends on registration order — module C stops receiving events because unrelated module B started throwing — a coupling that is invisible in code review and maddening in production. Catch Throwable rather than Exception in the loop, since an Error from one handler should not take the bus down either, but re-throw truly fatal ones if your platform distinguishes them. THE LEAK. The bus holds STRONG references to every handler, and a lambda capturing `this` holds its enclosing object. A component that subscribes in its constructor and is then discarded without unsubscribing is immortal, along with everything it references — the textbook listener leak, and it is the number one production failure of hand-rolled buses. Three fixes, in order of preference: (1) make Subscription an AutoCloseable handle and tie its lifetime to the owner's (try-with-resources, a DI container's destroy callback, a component's onDestroy) — explicit, deterministic, and the right default; (2) WEAK REFERENCES to the handler, so a collected owner's subscription becomes a tombstone that dispatch skips and periodically prunes — attractive but treacherous, because an anonymous lambda with no other strong reference is collected IMMEDIATELY and the subscription silently stops working, which is why Guava deliberately dropped weak handlers; (3) scope-bound registries that are dropped wholesale when a request or session ends. Say which one you would ship and why the weak-reference option is a trap unless the caller is guaranteed to keep the handler alive."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "EVENT BUS vs DIRECT CALLS: the bus buys decoupling — publishers do not compile against subscribers, and new consumers attach without touching producer code — and sells traceability. With direct calls, 'what happens when an order is placed?' is one find-usages; with a bus it is a runtime question requiring a registry dump or good naming discipline. The honest guidance is that a bus pays off for genuine fan-out and cross-cutting concerns (auditing, metrics, cache invalidation) and is over-engineering for a single consumer, where it just adds indirection and a debugging tax. Interviewers like candidates who can argue AGAINST their own component. COPY-ON-WRITE vs LOCK-AND-COPY vs SYNCHRONIZED LIST: CopyOnWriteArrayList is the right default given read/write ratios and gives snapshot iteration for free; a manual lock-copy-release-iterate is the same semantic with less garbage when handler lists are large and churn often; a synchronized list with iteration under the lock is WRONG here because it holds a lock across user code. REFLECTION/ANNOTATION-BASED SUBSCRIBERS (@Subscribe on methods, register(object)) vs EXPLICIT LAMBDAS: annotations are ergonomic and are what Guava and Spring's event model do, but they cost reflection at registration, lose compile-time type safety, and make 'who subscribes to this?' even harder; explicit typed subscribe(Class, Consumer) keeps the compiler involved and is easier to reason about — pick explicit for a hand-rolled bus and note the trade. SYNC vs ASYNC AS DEFAULT: default synchronous (predictable, debuggable, transactional) with async opt-in per subscription; a bus-wide async default surprises callers who assumed their handler ran before publish returned. UNBOUNDED vs BOUNDED ASYNC QUEUE: unbounded is a memory leak under sustained load; bounded forces a rejection policy — block the publisher (backpressure, may deadlock if the publisher is itself a bus thread) or drop with a metric. WHEN TO OUTGROW IT: the moment you need delivery guarantees across process restarts, retries, dead-letter queues, ordering across consumers, or cross-service fan-out, you want Kafka/RabbitMQ/SNS-SQS, not a richer in-process bus — and the design boundary is worth stating explicitly, because a bus that grows durability features badly is the classic failed abstraction. Extensions that fit cleanly: handler priority (sort the snapshot by priority, documenting that equal priorities are unordered); sticky events for late subscribers (keep the last event per type, replay on subscribe); a dead-event hook (already in the code) to catch typo'd or orphaned event types; and per-handler metrics (latency, error rate) which the isolation wrapper is already the natural place for."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'A handler unsubscribes itself during dispatch — what happens?' — with a plain ArrayList, ConcurrentModificationException aborts the whole publish and the remaining handlers are skipped; with CopyOnWriteArrayList the iteration is over a snapshot, so the removal takes effect on the NEXT publish and this one completes. Never hold a lock while calling a handler. 'One handler throws — do the rest run?' — yes, because every invocation is individually try/catch'd and the throwable is routed to an exception handler with the event attached; otherwise delivery silently depends on registration order. 'A handler publishes another event — what is the delivery order?' — synchronous dispatch is re-entrant and therefore depth-first: the nested event's handlers all run before the remaining handlers of the outer event. If you need the outer event to finish first, queue nested publishes in a ThreadLocal deque and drain after the top-level dispatch. Also add a depth guard, because event cycles are easy to create. 'I subscribed to the base Event class and got nothing.' — the registry must look up the whole type hierarchy (class, superclasses, interfaces), not do an exact-class get; cache the computed type list per class since it never changes. 'How do I unsubscribe a lambda?' — you cannot reliably compare lambdas, so subscribe returns a Subscription handle whose close() removes it; APIs that ask you to pass the same lambda back fail silently. 'Why is my memory growing?' — the bus holds strong references to handlers and lambdas capture their owner; unsubscribed listeners are immortal. Tie subscriptions to owner lifecycle (AutoCloseable handles, container destroy hooks), and treat weak references as a trap since a lambda with no other strong reference gets collected immediately. 'Sync or async by default?' — sync, for predictability and debuggability, with async opt-in per subscription and a bounded executor queue plus an explicit rejection policy. 'When would you replace this with a message broker?' — as soon as you need durability, retries, dead-lettering, ordered consumer groups, or delivery across processes; an in-process bus deliberately promises none of those, and growing it into a half-broker is the failure mode to avoid."),
    ],
    "The in-process event bus looks like a twenty-line HashMap exercise and is actually a concurrency and lifecycle interview in disguise — which is exactly why interviewers like it. The first probe is the registry lookup: exact-class matching silently delivers nothing to a handler registered on a base type or interface, so the hierarchy walk (cached per class) is the correct primitive. Then come the three dispatch-loop mechanisms that separate a toy from a shippable component: copy-on-write or snapshot iteration so a handler can subscribe/unsubscribe mid-dispatch without a ConcurrentModificationException, per-handler try/catch so one throwing subscriber cannot abort the remaining seven, and an explicit answer on re-entrancy — synchronous dispatch is depth-first, and a nested publish interleaves before the outer event finishes. The closer is the listener leak: the bus holds strong references forever, so subscriptions need owner-bound lifecycles, and a candidate who names weak references AND explains why they backfire for lambdas is signalling real production scars.",
    [
      G("exact_class_lookup", "Routing with an exact-class map get, so handlers registered on a base class or interface never receive subclass events — and nothing errors.", "Look up the event's full type set (class, superclasses, implemented interfaces) and deliver to all matching handler lists; cache the computed type list per event class since it is immutable, and document a delivery order (most-specific first).", "registry.get(event.getClass()) as the only lookup; a base-Event audit handler that silently never fires; no dead-event hook to surface unmatched publishes."),
      G("mutation_during_dispatch", "Iterating a plain ArrayList of handlers during publish — so a handler that unsubscribes itself throws ConcurrentModificationException and aborts delivery to everyone after it — or holding a lock across handler invocation.", "Iterate a stable snapshot: CopyOnWriteArrayList (justified by the huge read/write ratio) or lock-copy-release-then-iterate; mutations apply to the next publish. Never hold a lock while invoking foreign handler code.", "for-each over a mutable handler list inside publish; synchronized(publish) around the dispatch loop; one-shot listeners crashing the bus; deadlocks when a handler subscribes."),
      G("no_error_isolation_or_lifecycle", "Letting a handler exception propagate out of publish (aborting the rest of the fan-out) and holding strong references to handlers with no unsubscribe path.", "Wrap each invocation in try/catch routing to an exception handler and continue the loop; return an AutoCloseable Subscription handle tied to the owner's lifecycle, and treat weak-reference handlers as a trap because unreferenced lambdas get collected immediately.", "publish() with no try/catch inside the loop; subscribers that stop receiving events after an unrelated module started throwing; subscribe() returning void; heap dumps dominated by listener lambdas holding dead components."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.eventbus, "In-process event bus class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── RETRY / BACKOFF UTILITY ──────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t2", 2, "Design a Retry / Backoff Utility", "design-retry-backoff",
    ["case-study", "resilience", "distributed", "strategy"],
    "A downstream service blips for two seconds. Ten thousand of your clients each retry three times with a fixed one-second delay — and every one of them retries at the SAME instant, because they all failed at the same instant. The service comes back up, is immediately hit by thirty thousand synchronized requests, and falls over again. Meanwhile one of your calls has been retrying a 400 Bad Request for ninety seconds, and a payment got charged twice. Design a retry utility that fixes all three.",
    "Three decisions carry this whole component, and none of them is the loop. WHAT is retryable: transient failures only — retrying a deterministic 4xx is not resilience, it is a bug with a delay. HOW LONG to wait: exponential backoff with FULL JITTER, wait = random(0, min(cap, base·2^n)), because un-jittered backoff keeps synchronized clients synchronized and reconverges them into a retry storm. WHEN to stop: an overall DEADLINE, not just an attempt count — three attempts with a 30-second timeout each is a 90-second call nobody upstream is still waiting for. Plus the precondition everyone skips: a retry is only safe if the operation is IDEMPOTENT.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a reusable component that executes a caller-supplied operation (a Callable/Supplier) and, when it fails in a way the caller classifies as RETRYABLE, waits and tries again until it succeeds, exhausts its attempt budget, or blows its overall deadline. The configuration surface: maximum attempts; a backoff strategy (fixed, linear, exponential, with a maximum per-wait cap and a jitter mode); a RETRY PREDICATE deciding which exceptions and which result values are retryable; an overall time budget/deadline for the entire call including waits; and hooks — onRetry(attempt, exception, delay) for logging and metrics, and a recovery/fallback path when all attempts are exhausted. Behaviours the interviewer will push on: retries must respect INTERRUPTION and cancellation (a shutdown must not be blocked by a sleeping retry loop); the final failure must surface the LAST exception, ideally with the earlier ones attached as suppressed so the failure story is not lost; and the utility must be usable both synchronously (blocking the caller thread) and asynchronously (scheduling the next attempt on a scheduler rather than sleeping a thread). Non-functional constraints that shape everything: retries MULTIPLY LOAD on a dependency that is failing precisely because it is overloaded, so the design must be biased toward giving up rather than trying harder; the total latency of a retried call must be bounded, because an upstream caller with its own timeout has already given up while your loop is still sleeping; and thousands of independent clients must not synchronize their retries. Explicitly a precondition, not a feature: the operation must be idempotent, or retrying a request whose response was merely lost will duplicate a side effect. State that upfront — it is the single most-skipped requirement in this design and the one that causes double charges."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "RETRYEXECUTOR is the orchestrator that owns the loop: attempt, classify, decide, wait, repeat. It must be a small, boring, thoroughly tested object because everything else in the system routes through it. RETRYPOLICY is the immutable configuration bundle — maxAttempts, the overall deadline/budget, the backoff strategy, and the retry predicate — kept separate from the executor so the same executor serves many call sites with different policies, and so policies can be declared once per dependency (a slow analytics call and a latency-critical auth call want opposite settings). RETRYPREDICATE answers the WHAT question: shouldRetry(Throwable) and, importantly, also shouldRetry(Result) because plenty of APIs signal transient failure with a status code rather than an exception — a 503 or a 429 is retryable, a 400 or a 403 is not, and an HTTP client that turns both into the same IOException has erased the distinction the predicate needs. BACKOFFSTRATEGY answers HOW LONG: delayFor(attempt) returning a Duration, with implementations for fixed, exponential, and exponential-with-jitter (full jitter, equal jitter, decorrelated jitter — name them, they are the AWS Architecture Blog taxonomy every interviewer in this space has read). DEADLINE (or RetryBudget) is a first-class object holding the absolute end instant, consulted before every attempt AND before every sleep, and used to CLAMP the final sleep so the loop never overshoots. SLEEPER is a thin injectable abstraction over Thread.sleep or a ScheduledExecutorService — injectable specifically so tests can run a hundred simulated retries in microseconds instead of really sleeping, which is the difference between a tested retry utility and a hoped-for one. CIRCUITBREAKER is the neighbouring component the executor consults: if the breaker is OPEN, the attempt is skipped entirely and the call fails fast. RETRYCONTEXT carries per-call state — attempt number, first failure time, accumulated exceptions — for hooks and for the final composite error."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "STRATEGY is the obvious pattern and the interesting part is that there are TWO orthogonal strategies, not one: the retry PREDICATE (what) and the BACKOFF (how long) vary independently, and collapsing them into a single 'retry policy' class with both concerns hard-coded is the design smell — an exponential-backoff-on-5xx policy and an exponential-backoff-on-timeout policy should share the backoff object. The executor is a TEMPLATE/DECORATOR: it wraps any Callable and returns the same type, so retry composes with other cross-cutting wrappers (timeout, circuit breaker, bulkhead, metrics) rather than being baked into each call site. Composition ORDER is a real design decision worth raising unprompted: the standard stack from the outside in is retry -> circuit breaker -> timeout -> the call, so that each ATTEMPT gets its own timeout (a per-attempt timeout is what stops one hung attempt eating the entire deadline) and the breaker sees each attempt as a distinct success/failure sample. Invert it — breaker outside retry — and the breaker sees one composite result per call and its failure statistics become meaningless. The DEADLINE is threaded through as an object rather than recomputed from a start time in three places, because the clamping rule (never sleep past the deadline; never start an attempt that cannot finish before it) must be applied consistently. Injectable CLOCK and SLEEPER are not optional niceties: a retry utility whose tests actually sleep is a retry utility whose edge cases are untested, and interviewers notice whether testability is designed in. Two things are deliberately NOT in this component: the retry loop must not decide idempotency for the caller (it cannot know), so the API should make the caller assert it — a dedicated method name, a required idempotency key parameter, or at minimum documentation that is impossible to miss; and the utility should never retry silently, because a system where retries are invisible has no way to notice that a dependency degraded from 1% to 40% failure while latency crept up — every retry emits a metric with the attempt number and the reason."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "interface BackoffStrategy { Duration delayFor(int attempt); }\n\n/** FULL JITTER (AWS): wait = random(0, min(cap, base * 2^attempt)). */\nclass FullJitterBackoff implements BackoffStrategy {\n  private final long baseMs, capMs; private final Random rng;\n  FullJitterBackoff(long baseMs, long capMs, Random rng) {\n    this.baseMs = baseMs; this.capMs = capMs; this.rng = rng;\n  }\n  public Duration delayFor(int attempt) {\n    long exp = Math.min(capMs, baseMs * (1L << Math.min(attempt, 30)));  // no overflow\n    return Duration.ofMillis(rng.nextLong(exp + 1));   // uniform in [0, exp]\n  }\n}\n\nclass RetryPolicy {\n  final int maxAttempts; final Duration budget;\n  final BackoffStrategy backoff; final Predicate<Throwable> retryable;\n  RetryPolicy(int maxAttempts, Duration budget, BackoffStrategy b, Predicate<Throwable> r) {\n    this.maxAttempts = maxAttempts; this.budget = budget; this.backoff = b; this.retryable = r;\n  }\n}\n\nclass RetryExecutor {\n  private final Clock clock; private final Sleeper sleeper;\n  private final CircuitBreaker breaker;          // may be a no-op\n  private final BiConsumer<Integer, Throwable> onRetry;\n\n  <T> T execute(Callable<T> op, RetryPolicy p) throws Exception {\n    Instant deadline = clock.instant().plus(p.budget);   // TOTAL time, not per attempt\n    Throwable last = null;\n\n    for (int attempt = 0; attempt < p.maxAttempts; attempt++) {\n      if (!breaker.allowRequest())                        // dead dependency: fail fast\n        throw new CircuitOpenException(last);\n      try {\n        T result = op.call();\n        breaker.recordSuccess();\n        return result;\n      } catch (InterruptedException ie) {\n        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        throw ie;                                         // cancellation is NOT a retry\n      } catch (Throwable t) {\n        breaker.recordFailure();\n        if (last != null) t.addSuppressed(last);           // keep the failure story\n        last = t;\n        if (!p.retryable.test(t)) throw asException(t);     // PERMANENT: stop now\n        if (attempt == p.maxAttempts - 1) break;\n\n        Duration wait = p.backoff.delayFor(attempt);\n        Instant wakeAt = clock.instant().plus(wait);\n        if (!wakeAt.isBefore(deadline))                    // would overshoot the budget\n          throw new RetryBudgetExhausted(last);\n        onRetry.accept(attempt + 1, t);                    // ALWAYS observable\n        sleeper.sleep(wait);                               // interruptible\n      }\n    }\n    throw new RetriesExhausted(p.maxAttempts, last);\n  }\n}\n\n/** The predicate is where 'retrying a 400' becomes impossible. */\nclass HttpRetryPredicate implements Predicate<Throwable> {\n  public boolean test(Throwable t) {\n    if (t instanceof SocketTimeoutException) return true;      // transient\n    if (t instanceof ConnectException)       return true;      // transient\n    if (t instanceof HttpStatusException h) {\n      int s = h.status();\n      return s == 429 || s == 503 || s == 504 || (s >= 500 && s < 600);\n    }\n    return false;                                              // 4xx, parse errors: NO\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: what is retryable, and why full jitter is not optional",
        "WHAT TO RETRY is the decision that separates resilience from a bug amplifier. A retry is only sensible when the failure is TRANSIENT — the same request, repeated, has a genuine chance of succeeding. Connection refused during a rolling deploy, a socket timeout, a 503 from an overloaded node, a 429 rate-limit, a deadlock-victim rollback from a database, an S3 SlowDown: all transient. A 400 Bad Request, a 401, a 403, a 404, a JSON parse error, a null-pointer in your own mapping code: PERMANENT. Retrying a permanent failure is strictly worse than failing immediately — it burns the client's deadline, multiplies load on a dependency that is going to say no three more times, and hides the real error behind a RetriesExhausted wrapper so the operator sees 'timeout' instead of 'you sent a malformed field'. The design consequence is that classification must be an explicit, injected PREDICATE, not an accident of which exception type your HTTP client threw. And there is a nasty middle case worth raising unprompted: a 500 is usually transient, but a 500 caused by a deterministic bug in the downstream handler for YOUR payload will fail identically every time — which is why retry counts stay small (three attempts, not thirty) and why the circuit breaker exists as the second line of defence. The other middle case is a TIMEOUT: you do not know whether the server processed the request or not, which is precisely why idempotency is a precondition rather than a nicety. FULL JITTER. Consider ten thousand clients hitting a service that blips at T=0. Every client fails at T=0. With fixed backoff, every client retries at T=1 — ten thousand simultaneous requests. With plain exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s) they retry together at T=1, together at T=3, together at T=7: the backoff spread the retries over TIME but did nothing to spread them over each other, because they were synchronized by a common cause and deterministic delays PRESERVE synchronization. The service, still warming up, gets hit by a coordinated wave and fails again — and now the clients are re-synchronized by the new common failure, which is why this is a self-sustaining RETRY STORM (the thundering herd's distributed cousin). Randomization is the fix, and the exact formula matters. FULL JITTER: sleep = random(0, min(cap, base·2^n)) — the wait is uniform across the whole window, which maximally decorrelates clients; AWS's published measurements found it minimizes both total work and completion time versus the alternatives. EQUAL JITTER: sleep = half + random(0, half) — keeps a guaranteed minimum wait (useful when a too-short retry is certain to fail) at the cost of less spread. DECORRELATED JITTER: sleep = min(cap, random(base, prev·3)) — a random walk that spreads well while trending upward. The one to avoid is 'exponential plus or minus 10%', which sounds jittered and is not: the distributions still overlap tightly and the herd survives. The interviewer's probe here is usually 'why not just add a small random offset?' — the answer is that the spread must be proportional to the window, not a decoration on it. Also cap the exponential (otherwise attempt 20 sleeps for a fortnight) and guard the shift against overflow, which the code does with Math.min(attempt, 30)."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: deadlines over attempt counts, idempotency, and the circuit breaker interaction",
        "THE DEADLINE IS THE REAL BUDGET. Configuring 'three retries' and nothing else is the most common production mistake in this component, because attempts say nothing about time. Three attempts against a dependency with a 30-second timeout is a 90-second call, plus backoff — and the upstream caller, whose own timeout was 5 seconds, gave up 85 seconds ago. Every one of those seconds is a thread (or a connection, or a request slot) held for a response nobody will read. Worse, retry layers COMPOUND multiplicatively when they nest: if your service retries three times and the service it calls also retries three times, one user request can become nine downstream calls — and a four-layer stack turns one request into eighty-one. This is how a small blip becomes a total outage, and it is why mature systems either retry at exactly ONE layer (usually the outermost, closest to the user's actual deadline) or use a retry BUDGET — an amount of extra load, expressed as a percentage of base traffic, that the whole client is allowed to generate (Google's SRE book puts it at about 10%; when the budget is spent, retries are dropped even if attempts remain). Both patterns are worth naming. Concretely: thread an absolute deadline through the call, check it before starting each attempt (never start an attempt that cannot complete before the deadline), and CLAMP the sleep so the loop never wakes up past it. Then pass the remaining time down as the per-attempt timeout, which is the whole idea behind deadline propagation in gRPC and is the answer to 'how do these compose across services?'. IDEMPOTENCY IS THE PRECONDITION. When a call times out, the request may have been fully processed and only the response lost — retrying it repeats the side effect. For a GET or a PUT this is harmless by construction. For 'charge this card' or 'create this order' it is a duplicate charge, and no amount of clever backoff prevents it. The fix belongs to the OPERATION, not to the retry loop: attach an idempotency key generated once per logical operation (not once per attempt — regenerating the key per attempt defeats the whole mechanism, and this is the specific bug interviewers ask about), and have the server deduplicate on it, returning the original response for a repeat. The retry utility's job is to make this impossible to forget: require the caller to declare the operation idempotent, and default to NOT retrying non-idempotent operations. Say out loud that a retry utility applied to a non-idempotent operation is a correctness bug, not a resilience feature. THE CIRCUIT BREAKER handles the failure mode retry cannot: a dependency that is DOWN rather than blipping. Retry's implicit assumption is that the problem is brief; when it is not, every client politely hammering a dead service with exponentially-backed-off requests still adds up to sustained load on something trying to recover, and every caller pays the full deadline before failing. The breaker tracks the failure rate over a rolling window and, when it crosses a threshold, OPENS — subsequent calls fail immediately without an attempt — then after a cooldown moves to HALF-OPEN and lets a small number of probe requests through, closing if they succeed and re-opening if they fail. The composition rule, which is what gets probed: put the breaker INSIDE the retry loop so each attempt is a distinct sample, and check allowRequest() before each attempt — otherwise the loop keeps retrying against an open breaker, which is both pointless and a great way to trip your own metrics. Retry handles blips, the breaker handles outages, per-attempt timeouts handle hangs, and a bulkhead handles resource exhaustion: naming that division of labour cleanly is the strongest close available on this question."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "SYNCHRONOUS (thread-blocking) vs ASYNCHRONOUS (scheduled) RETRIES: the loop above sleeps a thread, which is simple and fine for a small pool of I/O calls, but at scale it pins one thread per in-flight retry — sleeping threads holding a connection each is how a service exhausts its pool while doing no work. The async form returns a CompletableFuture and schedules the next attempt on a ScheduledExecutorService, holding no thread during backoff; it is strictly better under load and strictly harder to reason about (exception handling and cancellation get subtle). Pick sync for simplicity, name async as the scaling answer. WHERE TO RETRY: retrying at every layer multiplies load geometrically; retrying only at the outermost layer knows the user's true deadline but cannot distinguish which hop failed; retrying only at the innermost sees the failure precisely but is invisible to the caller's deadline. The defensible answer is 'retry at one layer, propagate deadlines everywhere else' plus a retry budget to bound the amplification. FIXED vs EXPONENTIAL vs JITTERED: fixed is acceptable only for a single client with an uncontended resource; plain exponential fixes overload but not synchronization; full jitter fixes both and is the default; equal jitter is preferable when a minimum wait genuinely helps (a leader election settling, a lock being released). ATTEMPT COUNT vs TIME BUDGET vs BOTH: attempts alone unbound latency; a budget alone can burn itself on many fast failures; use both, with the budget authoritative. CLIENT-SIDE JITTER vs SERVER-SIGNALLED BACKOFF: if the server sends Retry-After (429/503) or a gRPC push-back, OBEY IT — server-side knowledge beats client heuristics, and ignoring Retry-After while claiming to implement backoff is a contradiction an interviewer may test. BUILD vs USE A LIBRARY: Resilience4j, Failsafe, Spring Retry and Polly all implement this correctly with breakers, bulkheads and rate limiters attached; hand-rolling is justified only for a trivial case or in an interview — say so, because the senior answer to 'write a retry utility' includes knowing you would normally not. Extensions: per-dependency policies loaded from config so backoff can be tuned without a deploy; retry-after honouring; hedged requests (fire a second request after p95 rather than waiting for failure — great for tail latency, doubles load, requires idempotency); and metrics that separate first-attempt failures from retried successes, because a service that looks 100% healthy because retries are hiding a 30% failure rate is a real and common blind spot."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Which failures do you retry?' — transient only: connection refused, timeouts, 429, 503/504, deadlock victims. Never 400/401/403/404 or parse errors; retrying a deterministic failure burns the deadline, multiplies load and hides the real error behind a wrapper. Classification lives in an injected predicate, not in whichever exception the HTTP client happened to throw. 'Why jitter — is exponential not enough?' — clients that failed together retry together; deterministic delays preserve that synchronization, so the herd reconverges on every wave. Full jitter, random(0, min(cap, base·2^n)), spreads uniformly across the whole window; a small random offset does not decorrelate. 'Three retries or a deadline?' — a deadline, with attempts as a secondary cap: three attempts times a 30-second timeout is a 90-second call the caller abandoned long ago. Clamp the sleep to the deadline and pass the remaining time down as each attempt's timeout. 'What if the retried call had already succeeded?' — that is the timeout case, and it is why retries require IDEMPOTENCY: one idempotency key per logical operation (never regenerated per attempt), deduplicated server-side. A retry utility on a non-idempotent operation is a correctness bug. 'What happens when the dependency is fully down?' — retry is the wrong tool; a circuit breaker opens after a failure-rate threshold, fails fast, and half-opens with probes. Check the breaker INSIDE the loop before each attempt so each attempt is one sample. 'My service retries and the service it calls also retries.' — amplification is multiplicative (3 x 3 = 9 calls per request); retry at one layer, propagate deadlines, and enforce a retry budget capping retry traffic at roughly 10% of base load. 'How do you test it?' — inject Clock and Sleeper so a hundred simulated attempts run instantly; assert the delay sequence, the deadline clamp, that permanent errors are not retried, and that interruption propagates. 'Make it non-blocking.' — return a CompletableFuture and schedule the next attempt on a ScheduledExecutorService instead of sleeping, so backoff holds no thread."),
    ],
    "The retry utility is a favourite senior screen because the code is fifteen lines and the judgment is unbounded — the interviewer is checking whether you have been on the wrong side of a retry storm. Three probes carry the question. WHAT to retry: a candidate who retries every exception is amplifying bugs, and the answer is an injected predicate that distinguishes transient (timeouts, 429, 503) from permanent (4xx, parse errors). HOW LONG: plain exponential backoff is the answer that sounds right and fails, because clients synchronized by a common failure stay synchronized under deterministic delays — full jitter, random(0, min(cap, base·2^n)), is the correct primitive and being able to name equal and decorrelated jitter as alternatives marks someone who has read the AWS material. WHEN to stop: a total deadline rather than an attempt count, plus awareness that nested retry layers multiply load geometrically and need a retry budget. The closer is idempotency — retries are only safe on operations that tolerate duplication, with one key per logical operation — and the division of labour with a circuit breaker for dependencies that are down rather than blipping.",
    [
      G("retry_everything", "Retrying every exception, including deterministic 4xx responses, validation failures and parse errors — turning a fast, clear failure into a slow, obscured one.", "Inject an explicit retry predicate that classifies transient (connect/read timeout, 429, 503/504, deadlock victim) from permanent (400/401/403/404, parse and mapping errors), and fail fast on the latter with the original exception preserved.", "catch (Exception e) { retry; } with no classification; RetriesExhausted wrappers hiding a 400 in production logs; retry counts configured but no predicate anywhere in the config surface."),
      G("unjittered_backoff", "Fixed or plain exponential delays with no randomization (or a token ±10% wobble), so clients that failed together retry together and reconverge into a retry storm.", "Use full jitter — sleep = random(0, min(cap, base·2^attempt)) — so waits spread uniformly across the whole window and clients decorrelate; cap the exponential, guard the shift against overflow, and honour a server-sent Retry-After when present.", "Thread.sleep(1000 * attempt) with no RNG; a step-shaped downstream QPS graph after any blip; backoff config with base and cap but no jitter mode; ignoring Retry-After headers."),
      G("attempts_without_deadline_or_idempotency", "Bounding retries only by attempt count — so a call can run far past the caller's timeout — and retrying non-idempotent operations, or regenerating the idempotency key per attempt.", "Thread an absolute deadline through the call: check it before each attempt, clamp the sleep so the loop never overshoots, and pass the remaining time as the per-attempt timeout. Require idempotency, with one key generated per logical operation and deduplicated server-side; layer a circuit breaker inside the loop for dependencies that are down.", "maxAttempts as the only bound; p99 latency equal to attempts x per-attempt timeout; idempotency key created inside the retry loop; duplicate charges/orders after a downstream timeout; retry logic at every layer of the call stack."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.retry, "Retry and backoff utility class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── MEMOIZATION / FUNCTION CACHE ─────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t3", 3, "Design a Memoization / Function Cache", "design-memoization",
    ["case-study", "concurrency", "caching", "generics"],
    "You wrap a 400ms pricing function in a HashMap cache. It works — until fifty threads ask for the same uncached key at the same millisecond and you run the 400ms computation fifty times, because all fifty found the map empty. You fix that with a synchronized block and now every cached lookup serializes behind one lock. You switch to computeIfAbsent and a recursive memoized function deadlocks the map. And the cache, unbounded, eats the heap. Design a memoization utility that survives all four.",
    "The mechanism that carries this problem is the COMPUTE-ONCE contract under concurrency, and the correct primitive is to cache a FUTURE, not a value: putIfAbsent an unstarted FutureTask, and whoever wins runs it while every loser blocks on get() — N concurrent callers, ONE computation. Everything else is precision work: the key must have correct equals/hashCode and must not be a mutable object you later modify; the cache must be BOUNDED (LRU) with optional TTL or it is a memory leak with a nice name; and the recursion subtlety that catches everyone — memoizing a recursive function only works if the function calls the MEMOIZED version, not itself.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a generic decorator that takes a pure function Function<K,V> (or a multi-argument function reduced to one key) and returns a function with identical semantics that computes each distinct input at most once, serving subsequent calls from a cache. It must be usable as a drop-in wrapper — memoize(this::expensiveCall) — so the call site does not change. Configuration: a maximum SIZE with an eviction policy (LRU by default), an optional TTL after write and/or after access, an optional refresh-ahead mode, and statistics (hit rate, load count, load latency, eviction count) because a cache whose hit rate nobody can see is a cache nobody can tune. Operations beyond get: explicit invalidate(key) and invalidateAll for correctness when the underlying data changes, and a way to pre-populate. Non-functional constraints that are the actual design: THREAD SAFETY with a compute-once guarantee — concurrent callers for a missing key must trigger exactly one computation, not one per caller, which is the difference between a cache that absorbs a load spike and one that amplifies it; the fast path (a hit) must not serialize on a global lock, or the cache becomes a bottleneck worse than the function it protects; BOUNDEDNESS, because an unbounded memoization cache on an unbounded key space is a slow memory leak that OOMs in week three; and CORRECT KEYING, because two calls with equal arguments must hit the same entry, which is entirely a question of equals/hashCode. Also state the applicability precondition loudly, since it is the requirement people skip: memoization is only valid for PURE functions — deterministic, side-effect-free, and stable over the entry's lifetime. Memoizing a function that reads mutable state or the clock caches a lie."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "MEMOIZER<K,V> is the decorator: it holds the wrapped function and the cache, and exposes apply(K). The whole point of making it a decorator rather than a base class is that the caller's type does not change — it is still a Function — so memoization can be added and removed without touching call sites, and can be composed with other decorators (metrics, retry). The CACHE MAP is a ConcurrentHashMap<K, Future<V>> — and the value type is the single most important decision in the design: mapping keys to VALUES cannot express 'this key is currently being computed', which is exactly the state that lets a stampede happen; mapping to a FUTURE makes the in-flight computation itself the cached entity, so a second caller finds it and waits rather than starting a duplicate. FUTURETASK<V> is the compute-once token: constructed unstarted, published atomically by putIfAbsent, and run only by the thread that won the race — every other thread calls get() and blocks on the same result. KEYBUILDER matters when the function has more than one argument: a key must be an immutable value with correct equals/hashCode, so either a record/data class per function or a List.of(args) wrapper; the naive string-concatenation key (\"a\" + \"b\" colliding with \"ab\" + \"\") is a real correctness bug worth naming. EVICTIONPOLICY owns boundedness: an LRU built on LinkedHashMap with accessOrder and removeEldestEntry (correct but requiring external synchronization, which conflicts with the lock-free read goal) or, in practice, a segmented/sampled policy — which is why the honest answer at this point is Caffeine, whose Window-TinyLFU beats hand-rolled LRU on hit rate and does eviction off the critical path. EXPIRYPOLICY handles TTL: an expireAfterWrite timestamp checked on read plus lazy eviction, or a scheduled cleaner. STATS is a small counter set (hits, misses, loads, load time, evictions) with concurrent adders."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "DECORATOR is the structural pattern and it is chosen deliberately: memoization is a cross-cutting behaviour layered onto a function, and any design that requires the wrapped code to know it is cached (a cache lookup inside the business method) has failed the extensibility test that the question is really asking. The core mechanism is the CACHE-AS-FUTURE idiom, and the reason it is worth naming as a pattern is that it converts a two-step check-then-compute into a single atomic publish: putIfAbsent(key, unstartedTask) either installs YOUR task (you compute) or returns the task someone else installed (you wait). There is no window between checking and computing, which is precisely the window a stampede lives in. Java hands you a shorter version of the same idea — ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent — and the trade between them is a genuinely good discussion point: computeIfAbsent is atomic and holds a BIN LOCK for the duration of the mapping function, which means (a) it blocks other writes to the same bin during a long computation, and (b) it explicitly forbids the mapping function from modifying the same map, which makes recursive memoization through computeIfAbsent an IllegalStateException or, in older JDKs, an infinite loop or corrupted table. The FutureTask form holds no map lock while computing — the map operation is just a putIfAbsent of an unstarted task — so long computations do not block unrelated writers and recursion works. Say that explicitly: computeIfAbsent for short, non-recursive loads; FutureTask-in-map for long or recursive ones. Two more choices: FAILURE HANDLING must not cache exceptions permanently — a failed load should be REMOVED from the map (cache.remove(key, future)) so the next caller retries, otherwise one transient blip is memoized forever, which is the subtlest bug in this whole design; and the eviction policy should be pluggable but the honest default is 'wrap Caffeine', because a hand-rolled LRU under concurrency is a lock on every read (LinkedHashMap must mutate its ordering on a GET) and that lock is exactly the bottleneck you were trying to avoid. Knowing that a strict-LRU concurrent cache needs either a global lock or an elaborate ring-buffer-of-reads design is the insight that makes 'use a library' a senior answer rather than a dodge."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "/** Compute-once memoizer: N concurrent callers, ONE computation. */\nclass Memoizer<K, V> implements Function<K, V> {\n  private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, Future<V>> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  private final Function<K, V> fn;\n\n  Memoizer(Function<K, V> fn) { this.fn = fn; }\n\n  @Override public V apply(K key) {\n    while (true) {\n      Future<V> f = cache.get(key);                 // fast path: no lock on a hit\n      if (f == null) {\n        FutureTask<V> task = new FutureTask<>(() -> fn.apply(key));  // NOT started yet\n        f = cache.putIfAbsent(key, task);           // atomic publish of the in-flight work\n        if (f == null) { f = task; task.run(); }    // we won: we compute\n      }                                             // we lost: we wait on THEIR future\n      try {\n        return f.get();                             // every loser blocks here\n      } catch (CancellationException e) {\n        cache.remove(key, f);                       // retry with a fresh task\n      } catch (ExecutionException e) {\n        cache.remove(key, f);                       // NEVER cache a failure forever\n        throw launder(e.getCause());\n      } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        throw new CancellationException();\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/** Multi-arg keys: an immutable record, never string concatenation. */\nrecord PriceKey(String sku, String region, int qty) { }   // equals/hashCode generated\n\n/** Recursion: the function must call the MEMOIZED version, not itself. */\nclass Fib {\n  private final Map<Integer, Long> memo = new HashMap<>();\n\n  long fib(int n) {                       // single-threaded: plain map is fine\n    if (n < 2) return n;\n    Long hit = memo.get(n);\n    if (hit != null) return hit;\n    long v = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);      // <-- recurses into the MEMOIZED method\n    memo.put(n, v);\n    return v;\n  }\n}\n\n/** Bounded + TTL: the honest production answer is a library. */\nclass BoundedMemoizer<K, V> {\n  private final LoadingCache<K, V> cache;\n  BoundedMemoizer(Function<K, V> fn, int maxSize, Duration ttl) {\n    cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()\n        .maximumSize(maxSize)              // bounded: not a memory leak\n        .expireAfterWrite(ttl)             // staleness bound\n        .recordStats()                     // hit rate is tunable only if measured\n        .build(fn::apply);                 // per-key compute-once, built in\n  }\n  V get(K k) { return cache.get(k); }\n  void invalidate(K k) { cache.invalidate(k); }\n}\n\n/** Hand-rolled LRU (single-threaded or externally synchronized). */\nclass LruCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {\n  private final int cap;\n  LruCache(int cap) { super(16, 0.75f, true); this.cap = cap; }   // accessOrder = true\n  @Override protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> e) { return size() > cap; }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the compute-once contract — caching futures, not values",
        "Walk the four versions in order, because the interviewer is grading whether you can see why each fails. VERSION ONE: a HashMap, get-then-compute-then-put. Broken on two axes — HashMap is not thread-safe (concurrent resize can corrupt the table into an infinite loop, a legendary production incident shape), and the check-then-act window means two callers both miss and both compute. VERSION TWO: synchronize the whole method. Now it is correct and useless: every HIT — the common case, the entire point of the cache — serializes on one lock, and worse, the lock is held DURING the 400ms computation, so a single cache miss blocks every other thread's hits for 400ms. Throughput is now lower than with no cache at all. VERSION THREE: ConcurrentHashMap of key to value. The hit path is finally fast and lock-free, and this version is where most candidates stop. It is still wrong under load, and the failure has a name: CACHE STAMPEDE (or dog-piling, or thundering herd). Fifty threads request an uncached hot key in the same millisecond; all fifty call get(), all fifty see null, all fifty run the 400ms computation, and all fifty put. The result is correct but you have just sent fifty identical queries to the database at the exact moment of peak demand — which is how a cache expiry on a hot key takes down the backend it was installed to protect. The map cannot help you here because it has no way to represent 'someone is already computing this'. VERSION FOUR fixes precisely that by changing what is cached: the map holds Future<V> rather than V, and a FutureTask is constructed UNSTARTED and published with putIfAbsent. putIfAbsent is atomic, so exactly one thread's task is installed; that thread calls run() and computes; every other thread receives the installed future from putIfAbsent (or from the subsequent get) and blocks on f.get(), waking with the same result when the winner finishes. Fifty callers, one computation, and no lock on the hit path — the entry itself becomes the synchronization point, which is the elegant part worth articulating. Three details that separate a memorized answer from an understood one. First, the outer while(true) loop plus the CancellationException handler: if the computing thread is cancelled, the cached future is poisoned, so remove it (with the two-arg remove, so you only remove YOUR stale future and not a replacement someone else installed) and loop to retry. Second, EXECUTIONEXCEPTION MUST EVICT: if you leave a failed future in the map, a transient failure is cached permanently and the key is dead for the life of the process — every subsequent caller re-throws the original exception with no chance of recovery. Third, the same guarantee is available for free from ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent and from Caffeine/Guava's LoadingCache, both of which document per-key compute-once; the difference is that computeIfAbsent holds a bin lock during the mapping function, which is fine for a 50-microsecond load and harmful for a 400ms one — and is fatal for recursion, since the mapping function may not modify the map. Being able to state that specific boundary is the payoff of understanding the mechanism rather than reciting the idiom."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: keying, boundedness and the recursion subtlety",
        "KEYING is where memoization silently returns wrong answers. The cache is a HashMap, so the key contract is equals/hashCode and everything follows from it. A key class without equals/hashCode uses identity, so two structurally identical requests miss every time and the hit rate is zero — a cache that quietly does nothing but consume memory. Mutable keys are worse: put an entry with a key object, then mutate a field of that object, and its hashCode changes, so it now hashes to a different bucket — the entry is unreachable AND undeletable, a leak plus a phantom miss. Keys must be IMMUTABLE VALUE OBJECTS — records, or defensive copies. For MULTI-ARGUMENT functions the reflex is to concatenate arguments into a string, and that is a genuine collision bug: key(\"ab\", \"c\") and key(\"a\", \"bc\") both produce \"abc\", so two different inputs share one cache entry and one of them gets the other's answer. Use a record or List.of(args); if you must build a string, use an unambiguous separator that cannot appear in the arguments — and note that the record is also faster, since it avoids the string building on every lookup. Varargs need the same treatment, and arrays are a trap because array equals is identity — wrap with List.of or Arrays.asList. Also: null keys and null RESULTS both need a decision, because a null value is indistinguishable from a miss in a plain map; either forbid nulls or wrap results in an Optional/sentinel, and say which. BOUNDEDNESS. An unbounded memoization cache is only safe when the key space is small and finite — memoizing fib(n) for n < 100 is fine; memoizing a user-supplied search string is a slow OOM, and it is a leak that passes every test because tests use ten distinct keys. Bound it: LRU is the default policy, implemented via LinkedHashMap with accessOrder=true and removeEldestEntry, which is exactly right for single-threaded use and awkward concurrently — a GET mutates the recency order, so every read needs the write lock, destroying the lock-free hit path. That is the concrete reason production caches (Caffeine's Window-TinyLFU, or a CLOCK/second-chance approximation) buffer reads and do eviction off the critical path, and it is why 'use Caffeine' is the correct engineering answer while 'here is LinkedHashMap' is the correct whiteboard answer — give both. TTL is the orthogonal bound: a size limit bounds memory, expireAfterWrite bounds STALENESS, and they solve different problems. Memoization strictly assumes purity, so TTL only enters when the function is pure-ish — stable for a while but not forever (a price, a config value) — and that is also when explicit invalidate(key) earns its place. THE RECURSION SUBTLETY, which is the classic gotcha: wrapping a recursive function from the outside memoizes only the OUTERMOST call. memoize(this::fib) caches fib(50), but the internal fib(n-1) + fib(n-2) calls go to the raw method, so the exponential tree underneath is fully re-explored — the wrapper appears to do nothing on the first call and the candidate concludes memoization does not work. The fix is that recursion must route through the memoized version: either the method looks in its own memo table (the Fib class above), or the function is written to take its memoized self as a parameter — Function<Integer,Long> self, with a fixed-point wiring — which is how you memoize a lambda you cannot edit. State the general rule: memoization must intercept EVERY recursive call, not just the entry point."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "HAND-ROLLED vs LIBRARY: the FutureTask memoizer is ~25 lines and correct for compute-once, but a production cache also wants bounded size with a good eviction policy, TTL, refresh-ahead, stats, and weak/soft value options — and each of those is subtle under concurrency (strict LRU needs a lock on every read; correct TTL needs lazy expiry plus a cleanup path). Caffeine solves all of it with better hit rates than LRU; Guava Cache is the older sibling; a JCache/Redis layer is the answer when the cache must be shared across instances. The senior answer is to build the mechanism on the whiteboard and say you would ship the library, with the reason (concurrent eviction is where hand-rolled caches go wrong), not just the name. COMPUTEIFABSENT vs FUTURETASK: computeIfAbsent is shorter, atomic, and idiomatic — use it for short loads; it holds a bin lock for the mapping function's duration and forbids recursive modification of the same map, so use the FutureTask form for long computations and anything recursive. That specific trade is the most likely follow-up in the whole topic. STRONG vs WEAK/SOFT VALUES: weak values let entries vanish as soon as nothing else references them (a natural fit for interning-style caches); soft values defer collection to memory pressure and are widely regarded as a GC-pressure trap; strong values plus an explicit size bound is the predictable default. CACHE FAILURES OR NOT: never cache exceptions indefinitely; optionally cache a NEGATIVE result (a real 'not found') with a short TTL, which is the standard defence against cache-penetration attacks that query non-existent keys — distinguishing 'the load failed' from 'the answer is genuinely absent' is a nice distinction to draw. PER-INSTANCE vs SHARED CACHE: a local memoizer is fast and duplicated across N instances (N x the load on a cold key, N x the memory); a shared Redis cache is consistent and adds a network hop plus a serialization contract, and turns invalidation into a distributed problem. TTL JITTER: if many entries are written at once with the same TTL they all expire at once and stampede together — the same synchronization pathology as un-jittered retry backoff, fixed the same way by randomizing the TTL slightly, and drawing that parallel between two topics is a strong close. Extensions: async loading (return CompletableFuture so callers do not block on a miss), refresh-ahead (serve the stale value while recomputing in the background, which trades a little staleness for zero miss latency on hot keys), and per-key stats to find the keys actually worth caching."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Fifty threads ask for the same missing key — how many times does the function run?' — once, if you cache a FUTURE: putIfAbsent an unstarted FutureTask, the winner runs it, the other forty-nine block on the same get(). A map of key-to-VALUE cannot express 'in flight', which is exactly why the stampede happens there. 'Why not just synchronize the method?' — it serializes the hit path (the common case) and holds the lock during the computation, so one miss blocks every hit for the duration; it is correct and slower than no cache. 'computeIfAbsent instead?' — same compute-once guarantee and shorter, but it holds a bin lock for the mapping function's duration and forbids modifying the same map inside it, so it is wrong for long loads and fatal for recursive memoization; the FutureTask form holds no map lock while computing. 'The function threw — what is cached?' — nothing: remove the failed future (with the two-arg remove so you do not evict a replacement) and let the next caller retry, or a transient blip is memoized permanently and the key is dead for the process lifetime. 'How do you key a three-argument function?' — an immutable record with generated equals/hashCode; string concatenation collides (\"ab\"+\"c\" equals \"a\"+\"bc\") and arrays compare by identity. Never mutate a key after insertion — its hashCode moves and the entry becomes unreachable and undeletable. 'I memoized my recursive Fibonacci and it is still exponential.' — the wrapper only intercepted the outer call; the internal recursion still calls the raw function. Recursion must go through the memoized version — an internal memo table or a self-referential Function parameter. 'The heap keeps growing.' — an unbounded cache on an unbounded key space is a leak; bound it with maximumSize plus LRU (or Caffeine's TinyLFU) and add a TTL if the function is only pure-ish. 'Everything expired at once and hammered the database.' — same shape as un-jittered retry: randomize the TTL, or use refresh-ahead so a hot key is recomputed in the background while the stale value is still served."),
    ],
    "Memoization is the deceptive interview: everyone can write get-or-compute in three lines, so the entire signal is in what happens next. The first probe is the cache stampede — fifty concurrent callers on a cold hot key — and the discriminating answer is to cache a FUTURE rather than a value so putIfAbsent of an unstarted FutureTask makes compute-once structural, with every loser blocking on the winner's result. Candidates who only reach ConcurrentHashMap-of-values have a correct cache that amplifies load exactly when the system is under stress. The second probe is keying: immutable records with real equals/hashCode, never concatenated strings (which collide) or mutable objects (whose hashCode moves and orphans the entry). The third is boundedness — unbounded memoization is a leak that passes every test — and the fourth is the recursion gotcha, where wrapping a recursive function from the outside memoizes only the entry point. Strong candidates also know never to cache a failure permanently, and can say exactly where computeIfAbsent stops being the right tool.",
    [
      G("cache_stampede", "Caching key-to-VALUE in a ConcurrentHashMap, so N concurrent callers all miss on a cold key and all run the expensive computation — or synchronizing the whole method and serializing every hit behind one lock held during the load.", "Cache a Future: construct an UNSTARTED FutureTask, publish it with putIfAbsent, let the winner run() it and every loser block on get(). One computation per key, no lock on the hit path — or use computeIfAbsent / a LoadingCache, which document the same per-key compute-once guarantee.", "map.get then compute then put with no atomic publish; N identical slow queries in the trace at the same millisecond after an expiry; synchronized around the whole memoized method; backend load spiking when a hot key expires."),
      G("bad_keys_and_cached_failures", "Keying on concatenated argument strings (or arrays, or mutable objects), and leaving a failed computation in the cache so one transient error is memoized forever.", "Key on an immutable record/value object with generated equals/hashCode — never string concatenation (\"ab\"+\"c\" collides with \"a\"+\"bc\") and never an object you later mutate; on ExecutionException remove the entry with the two-arg remove so the next caller retries, and cache negative results only deliberately with a short TTL.", "cache keys built with + or String.format; zero hit rate because the key class lacks equals/hashCode; an entry that can neither be found nor removed after a key field changed; one exception replayed to every caller for the life of the process."),
      G("unbounded_and_outer_only_memoization", "Leaving the cache unbounded on an open key space, and wrapping a recursive function from the outside so only the entry call is memoized while the internal recursion stays exponential.", "Bound the cache (maximumSize + LRU, or Caffeine's TinyLFU) and add a TTL when the function is only pure-ish, with jittered expiry so entries do not all expire together; make recursion call the MEMOIZED version — an internal memo table or a self-referential Function parameter — so every recursive call is intercepted, not just the first.", "a HashMap field that only ever grows; heap dumps dominated by one cache; memoize(this::fib) with fib recursing into itself; hit counts of exactly one per distinct top-level input."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.memoize, "Memoization function cache class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── CSV PARSER ───────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m16", "lld_m16_t4", 4, "Design a CSV Parser", "design-csv-parser",
    ["case-study", "state-machine", "streaming", "parsing"],
    "The row is: 1,\"Smith, John\",\"He said \"\"hi\"\"\",\"line one\\nline two\",,42. Your split(\",\") produces eight fields instead of six, puts a stray quote in the middle of one, and the embedded newline has already been chopped into two rows by your readLine loop. Now the file is 4GB and you were about to call Files.readAllLines. Design a CSV parser that gets all of this right.",
    "CSV is not a delimited format you can split — it is a small LANGUAGE, and RFC 4180 requires a STATE MACHINE. A quoted field makes commas and NEWLINES literal, so a line is not a record; a doubled quote inside a quoted field is an escaped quote, so the closing-quote decision requires one character of lookahead. Get that machine right and everything else is configuration: the dialect (delimiter, quote, escape, header), streaming row-by-row so a 4GB file never lands in memory, and an explicit policy for the ragged rows that real-world files always contain.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: read CSV text and produce records — each record a list of string fields, optionally mapped to a header row so fields are addressable by name. RFC 4180 conformance is the baseline and each clause is a design constraint: fields are separated by a delimiter; any field MAY be enclosed in double quotes; a field containing the delimiter, a quote character, or a line break MUST be quoted; a quote inside a quoted field is escaped by doubling it (\"\"); leading and trailing spaces are part of the field and must not be trimmed by default; records are separated by CRLF, but a real parser must accept LF and lone CR too, and must not require a trailing newline on the last record. Beyond the RFC, real files demand DIALECT configuration, because half the world's CSV is not RFC-conformant: alternative delimiters (semicolon in European locales, tab, pipe), an alternative quote character, backslash escaping instead of doubling, comment lines to skip, a configurable number of leading rows to skip, optional whitespace trimming, and a BOM to strip. Output-side features: a header mode that maps names to indices, optional type coercion (int, decimal, date, boolean, and a nullable/empty-string distinction), and an error policy. Non-functional constraints that drive the design: files are LARGE, so parsing must STREAM — constant memory per row, never load the file — and must be fast, so the hot loop should avoid per-character object allocation and regex; ERROR REPORTING must include line and column numbers and the offending row, because 'parse error' on row 2,000,000 of a nightly import is useless; and ENCODING is explicit (UTF-8 default, BOM handling), never the platform default. Round-tripping — a WRITER that quotes exactly when needed — should be mentioned as the natural companion, since the quoting rules are the same machine run backwards."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "CSVPARSER is the entry point and its shape is itself a design statement: it should implement Iterator<Row> or Iterable<Row> (or expose a Stream<Row>), because that is what makes streaming the DEFAULT rather than an option — an API returning List<Row> has already decided to load the file. It holds the reader, the dialect, and the tokenizer's state across the pull-based iteration. TOKENIZER is the heart: a character-driven FINITE STATE MACHINE with states START_OF_FIELD, IN_UNQUOTED_FIELD, IN_QUOTED_FIELD, QUOTE_IN_QUOTED_FIELD (the lookahead state that decides escaped-quote versus end-of-field), and END_OF_RECORD. It consumes characters from a buffered reader and emits fields; it owns a single reusable StringBuilder so a million-row parse does not allocate a million builders. DIALECT is the immutable configuration value object — delimiter, quote char, escape mode (doubling vs backslash), record separator handling, hasHeader, comment prefix, trim policy, strictness flag — and making it a first-class object is what stops the parser growing a constructor with nine booleans. RECORDASSEMBLER collects the tokenizer's fields into a Row and, in header mode, holds the name-to-index map so Row.get(\"email\") works without a per-row map allocation (share one immutable index map across all rows — allocating a HashMap per record is the classic performance mistake in this design). ROW is the record value: the field list, the record number and the source line number for error messages, plus typed accessors. ERRORPOLICY decides what a malformed or ragged record does: throw with position (strict), skip and collect (lenient with a report), or pad/truncate to the header width — an explicit strategy rather than a hard-coded choice, because bulk-import tooling and financial reconciliation want opposite behaviour. TYPECOERCER is optional and deliberately separate: parsing text into fields and interpreting fields as types are different jobs, and fusing them makes the parser untestable and the coercion rules unreusable."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The load-bearing structural decision is that the tokenizer is a STATE MACHINE over characters, and the parser is PULL-BASED. Both deserve justification. State machine, because CSV's grammar is context-sensitive at the character level: the meaning of a comma depends on whether you are inside quotes, and the meaning of a quote depends on both the current state and the NEXT character. Any implementation that starts by splitting on a delimiter has thrown away the state it needs and will spend the rest of its life adding special cases — the tell-tale symptom is a parser with a boolean inQuotes flag bolted onto a split-based loop, which is a state machine implemented badly. Write it as an explicit switch over an enum state or, faster, as a small loop with the state as a local variable; either way, name the states, because the interviewer wants to see that you know the transition table before you know the syntax. Pull-based iteration, because the parser must not decide what the caller does with the rows: an Iterator lets the caller filter, batch, abort early, and — critically — process a file larger than memory, whereas a callback/push design (parse(file, rowHandler)) makes early termination awkward and composition with streams impossible. Mention that push parsing is the natural fit if you are feeding a reactive pipeline and that the two are convertible, but default to pull. The DIALECT-as-value-object is the extensibility seam: new formats are new Dialect instances, not new parser subclasses, which is the difference between supporting semicolon-delimited European exports in one line and forking the parser. Separating the tokenizer from the record assembler from the type coercer follows the same reasoning as the coupon engine's rule/strategy split — each layer is independently testable, and the tokenizer in particular becomes a pure function from a character stream to a field stream that you can exhaustively unit test with the ugly inputs (quote at end of file, empty quoted field, quoted empty field versus missing field, CRLF inside quotes). What is deliberately absent: regex. A regex that 'handles CSV' exists, is unreadable, is slower than the hand-written machine, and cannot stream across buffer boundaries — being able to say why regex is the wrong tool here, rather than just avoiding it, is worth points."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "class Dialect {\n  final char delimiter, quote;\n  final boolean doubledQuoteEscape;    // \"\"\"\" style vs backslash\n  final boolean hasHeader, trimUnquoted;\n  Dialect(char d, char q, boolean dq, boolean hdr, boolean trim) {\n    delimiter = d; quote = q; doubledQuoteEscape = dq; hasHeader = hdr; trimUnquoted = trim;\n  }\n  static Dialect rfc4180() { return new Dialect(',', '\"', true, true, false); }\n  static Dialect europeanSemicolon() { return new Dialect(';', '\"', true, true, false); }\n}\n\nclass CsvParser implements Iterator<Row>, Closeable {\n  private enum S { FIELD_START, UNQUOTED, QUOTED, QUOTE_SEEN }\n  private final Reader in; private final Dialect d;\n  private final StringBuilder field = new StringBuilder();   // ONE reusable builder\n  private List<String> header; private long lineNo = 1, recordNo = 0;\n  private Row next; private boolean eof;\n\n  CsvParser(Reader in, Dialect d) throws IOException {\n    this.in = new BufferedReader(in); this.d = d;\n    if (d.hasHeader) { Row h = readRecord(); header = h == null ? List.of() : h.fields(); }\n    next = readRecord();\n  }\n\n  public boolean hasNext() { return next != null; }\n  public Row next() {\n    Row r = next;\n    try { next = readRecord(); } catch (IOException e) { throw new UncheckedIOException(e); }\n    return r;\n  }\n\n  /** The state machine. Streams one record; the file is never fully read. */\n  private Row readRecord() throws IOException {\n    if (eof) return null;\n    List<String> fields = new ArrayList<>();\n    S state = S.FIELD_START;\n    field.setLength(0);\n    long startLine = lineNo;\n    int c;\n    while ((c = in.read()) != -1) {\n      char ch = (char) c;\n      switch (state) {\n        case FIELD_START:\n          if (ch == d.quote)          { state = S.QUOTED; }\n          else if (ch == d.delimiter) { fields.add(\"\"); }\n          else if (ch == '\\n')        { fields.add(\"\"); lineNo++; return row(fields, startLine); }\n          else if (ch == '\\r')        { /* wait for the \\n of CRLF */ }\n          else                        { field.append(ch); state = S.UNQUOTED; }\n          break;\n        case UNQUOTED:\n          if (ch == d.delimiter)      { fields.add(take()); state = S.FIELD_START; }\n          else if (ch == '\\n')        { fields.add(take()); lineNo++; return row(fields, startLine); }\n          else if (ch == '\\r')        { /* CRLF: swallow */ }\n          else                        { field.append(ch); }\n          break;\n        case QUOTED:\n          // Inside quotes a delimiter and a NEWLINE are literal data.\n          if (ch == d.quote)          { state = S.QUOTE_SEEN; }\n          else                        { if (ch == '\\n') lineNo++; field.append(ch); }\n          break;\n        case QUOTE_SEEN:\n          // One char of lookahead decides: escaped quote or end of field?\n          if (ch == d.quote)          { field.append(d.quote); state = S.QUOTED; }  // \"\" -> \"\n          else if (ch == d.delimiter) { fields.add(take()); state = S.FIELD_START; }\n          else if (ch == '\\n')        { fields.add(take()); lineNo++; return row(fields, startLine); }\n          else if (ch == '\\r')        { /* CRLF after closing quote */ }\n          else throw new CsvException(\"stray character after closing quote\", lineNo);\n          break;\n      }\n    }\n    eof = true;\n    if (state == S.QUOTED) throw new CsvException(\"unterminated quoted field\", startLine);\n    if (state != S.FIELD_START || !fields.isEmpty()) { fields.add(take()); return row(fields, startLine); }\n    return null;                     // no trailing newline required\n  }\n\n  private String take() {\n    String s = field.toString(); field.setLength(0);\n    return d.trimUnquoted ? s.trim() : s;\n  }\n  private Row row(List<String> f, long line) { return new Row(++recordNo, line, f, header); }\n  public void close() throws IOException { in.close(); }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the RFC 4180 state machine, and exactly why split(\",\") is wrong",
        "Start with the failure, because it is vivid and universal. Given 1,\"Smith, John\",42 the naive line.split(\",\") returns [1, \"Smith, John, 42] — four fields, one of them a fragment with an orphan quote, and the record silently shifts every downstream column by one. Add \"He said \"\"hi\"\"\" and split produces garbage plus a doubled quote nobody unescaped. Add a quoted field containing a newline and the damage moves up a level: your readLine() loop has already cut ONE record into TWO before split ever sees it, which is why 'a line is a record' is the deepest wrong assumption in naive CSV handling. Then note the meta-point that makes the argument stick: these are not exotic inputs. Any address field contains a comma, any free-text comment contains quotes or newlines, and every spreadsheet export produces them — so a split-based parser does not fail on a hostile file, it fails on Tuesday. THE CORRECT PRIMITIVE is a character-level state machine with four working states, and the transition table IS the answer to this question — be able to recite it. FIELD_START: a quote opens a quoted field; a delimiter means this field was empty; a newline ends the record; anything else is the first character of an unquoted field. IN_UNQUOTED_FIELD: a delimiter ends the field; a newline ends the field and the record; everything else accumulates — note that per the RFC a quote appearing mid-unquoted-field is malformed, and your strictness policy decides whether to throw or accept it literally (most real parsers accept it, since exporters emit it). IN_QUOTED_FIELD: this is where the magic lives — a delimiter is LITERAL DATA, a newline is LITERAL DATA (and you increment the line counter for error messages while still staying in the same record), and only a quote character changes anything, moving to the lookahead state. QUOTE_SEEN: one character of lookahead resolves the ambiguity that makes CSV context-sensitive — another quote means the pair was an ESCAPED QUOTE, so append one quote and return to IN_QUOTED_FIELD; a delimiter means the quote CLOSED the field; a newline means it closed the field and the record; end-of-input means it closed the field and the file; anything else is malformed (a stray character after a closing quote). That single lookahead state is the crux of the whole design, and candidates who try to decide the closing quote without it end up with a mutually-recursive tangle of flags. THREE EDGE CASES worth volunteering, because they are what a test suite is made of: the distinction between an EMPTY FIELD (a,,b — a zero-length string) and a QUOTED EMPTY FIELD (a,\"\",b — also zero-length, but some tools use the difference to encode NULL versus empty, so the parser should be able to report whether a field was quoted); an UNTERMINATED QUOTED FIELD at end of file, which must be an error with the line where the quote OPENED, not the line where the file ended (the whole point of tracking startLine); and the absence of a trailing newline, which must still yield the final record — the code's post-loop flush handles it, and forgetting it silently drops the last row of every file, a bug that has shipped everywhere."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: streaming, dialects, type coercion and ragged rows",
        "STREAMING is a correctness requirement disguised as a performance one. Files.readAllLines or reading into a String on a 4GB file does not run slowly — it throws OutOfMemoryError, and it does so in production on the one file that matters, because the developer tested with a 500-row sample. The design fix is that the API's DEFAULT shape must be pull-based: implement Iterator<Row> (or return a Stream<Row>) over a BufferedReader, hold exactly one record in memory at a time, and let the caller decide about batching or collecting. Two supporting details: the state machine must work across buffer boundaries, which it does naturally because it consumes a character at a time from a BufferedReader rather than operating on a line or a block — an implementation that reads a chunk and parses it must handle a record straddling the chunk edge, which is a whole class of bugs the character-pull design never has; and the parser holds a SINGLE reusable StringBuilder plus a shared header index map, because allocating a builder and a HashMap per record is what makes hand-rolled parsers ten times slower than they need to be at a million rows. Also, being a Closeable that owns the reader gives the caller try-with-resources, which matters because an early-terminating stream must still release the file handle. DIALECTS are the difference between a parser and a toy. Real-world files use semicolons (European locales, where the comma is a decimal separator), tabs, or pipes; some escape quotes with a backslash instead of doubling; some carry a UTF-8 BOM that will otherwise appear inside your first header name and break every column lookup with an invisible character (a genuinely maddening bug worth naming); some have comment lines or preamble rows to skip; and encodings vary, so the charset must be explicit rather than the platform default — a file parsed as UTF-8 on the developer's machine and Windows-1252 on the server produces mojibake, not an error. Model all of this as an immutable Dialect value object passed to the parser, so support for a new source is a configuration line and not a subclass. TYPE COERCION belongs OUTSIDE the tokenizer. The parser's job ends at strings; converting \"1,234.50\" or \"2024-03-01\" or \"\" to a typed value involves locale, format patterns, and a null policy, and fusing it into the state machine makes both parts untestable. Keep it as a separate mapper layer, and note the empty-versus-null question explicitly, because in a database import 'empty string' and 'NULL' are different values and CSV cannot distinguish them without a convention. RAGGED ROWS are the last policy question and the most operationally important. Real files contain records with more or fewer fields than the header — a truncated export, an unescaped delimiter upstream, a manually edited file. The parser must not silently pad or silently drop, because both corrupt data quietly; it must apply an explicit ERROR POLICY: STRICT (throw a CsvException carrying record number, line number, expected versus actual field count, and the raw row text), LENIENT-COLLECT (yield the row anyway, record the problem in a report the caller can inspect afterwards, and keep going — the right default for a bulk import where you want all the good rows plus a rejects file), or NORMALIZE (pad missing fields with empty and/or truncate extras, only when the caller has explicitly opted in). Whichever you choose, the error must carry POSITION: 'CSV parse error' on row 2,000,000 without a line number and the offending text is an unactionable page at 3am, and saying that sentence tends to land with interviewers who have received it."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "HAND-ROLLED vs LIBRARY: the state machine is 60 lines and correct, but Apache Commons CSV, OpenCSV, Jackson's CSV module and univocity-parsers already handle dialects, BOMs, malformed-row policies and edge cases you will not think of, with univocity in particular being dramatically faster than naive implementations. The senior answer is to build the machine on the whiteboard and ship a library in production, with the reason: CSV's edge cases are a long tail discovered in production, not a hard algorithm. PULL (Iterator/Stream) vs PUSH (callback) API: pull composes with the caller's control flow, supports early termination and lazy evaluation, and is the better default; push is a natural fit for reactive pipelines and lets the parser own the loop (marginally faster, no state to suspend). Say which you would default to and why. STRICT vs LENIENT: strict fails fast and protects data integrity — right for financial or regulatory feeds; lenient with a rejects report maximizes throughput on messy bulk imports — right for user-uploaded files. This is a policy the CALLER must choose, so it belongs in configuration, not in the parser's opinion. CHARACTER-AT-A-TIME vs BLOCK PARSING: the character loop over a BufferedReader is simple, correct across boundaries, and fast enough for almost everything; reading into a char[] block and indexing directly avoids per-character method call overhead and is what the fast libraries do, at the cost of explicit boundary handling — a reasonable optimization to name but not to write first. PARALLEL PARSING: tempting for huge files and genuinely hard, because you cannot split a CSV file at an arbitrary byte offset — a newline inside a quoted field means you cannot know whether a given offset is inside or outside quotes without scanning from the start. The workable approaches are to parse sequentially and hand rows to a worker pool (usually enough, since the consumer is normally the bottleneck), or to speculatively split and validate, and knowing WHY naive splitting is unsound is exactly the kind of insight that separates candidates. Extensions: a matching WRITER that quotes a field exactly when it contains the delimiter, a quote or a newline (and doubles internal quotes) — the same rules run backwards, and round-trip property tests against the parser are the best test suite this component can have; header-to-object binding via annotations or a record mapper; on-the-fly decompression by wrapping the reader; and column-count/type inference for tooling."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Why not split on commas?' — because a comma inside a quoted field is data, so the meaning of a character depends on parser state; split throws that state away. And a newline inside a quoted field means a line is not a record, so even reading line by line before splitting has already corrupted the input. 'How do you know whether a quote closes the field or is an escaped quote?' — one character of lookahead: after seeing a quote inside a quoted field, another quote means an escaped quote (append one, stay quoted); a delimiter, newline or EOF means the field closed; anything else is malformed. That lookahead state is the crux of the machine. 'A field contains a newline — how many records?' — one: inside a quoted field the newline is literal data. Increment the line counter for error reporting but do not end the record; this is exactly why readLine-based parsing cannot be made correct. 'The file is 4GB.' — stream it: expose Iterator<Row> or Stream<Row> over a BufferedReader, one record in memory at a time, and never a method that returns List<Row>. The character-pull loop also removes the buffer-boundary problem that block parsing has. 'The file uses semicolons and backslash escapes.' — a Dialect value object (delimiter, quote, escape mode, header, comments, trim, charset), so a new source is configuration rather than a parser subclass; also strip the BOM, or it hides inside your first header name and breaks every lookup invisibly. 'A row has five fields but the header has six.' — an explicit error policy: strict throws with record number, line number, expected versus actual count and the raw text; lenient yields the row and records it in a rejects report; normalize pads or truncates only on explicit opt-in. Never silently pad. 'The last line has no newline.' — the post-loop flush must still emit that record; forgetting it silently drops the final row of every file. 'Can you parallelize it?' — not by splitting at byte offsets, because a quoted newline makes it impossible to know if an offset is inside quotes without scanning from the start; parse sequentially and fan the rows out to workers. 'Would you write this in production?' — no: Commons CSV, OpenCSV or univocity, because the edge cases are a long tail; the machine is worth knowing so you can debug them."),
    ],
    "The CSV parser is the interviewer's cleanest test of whether a candidate recognizes a parsing problem when it is disguised as string manipulation. The opening move decides the interview: reaching for split(\",\") — or for a readLine loop with an inQuotes boolean bolted on — signals that the candidate has not seen that quoted fields make the grammar context-sensitive, with commas and NEWLINES becoming literal data inside quotes and a doubled quote requiring one character of lookahead to disambiguate from a closing quote. The strong answer names the state machine and its transition table up front. The second probe is memory: an API that returns List<Row> has already failed the 4GB file, so streaming via Iterator/Stream over a BufferedReader must be the default shape rather than an option. The third is real-world messiness — configurable dialects (semicolons, backslash escapes, BOMs, encodings), a deliberate policy for ragged rows, and errors that carry line, column and the offending text. Candidates who volunteer the empty-versus-quoted-empty distinction, the unterminated-quote error, and the missing trailing newline are describing a test suite they have actually written.",
    [
      G("split_on_delimiter", "Parsing with line.split(\",\") — or reading line by line and bolting an inQuotes boolean onto a split loop — so quoted commas fragment fields and escaped quotes leak into the data.", "Implement the RFC 4180 state machine over characters: FIELD_START, IN_UNQUOTED, IN_QUOTED (delimiter and newline are literal here) and QUOTE_SEEN (one character of lookahead distinguishing an escaped \"\" from a closing quote); the tokenizer is a pure function from a character stream to fields and is exhaustively unit-testable.", "split(\",\") or a CSV regex in the parse path; a boolean inQuotes flag inside a line-based loop; addresses fragmenting into extra columns; a doubled quote surviving into the field value."),
      G("line_equals_record_and_load_all", "Assuming a line is a record — so a quoted embedded newline splits one record into two — and loading the whole file with readAllLines or returning List<Row>.", "Let the state machine own record boundaries (a newline inside IN_QUOTED is data; only increment the line counter), and stream: expose Iterator<Row>/Stream<Row> over a BufferedReader, one record in memory, Closeable so early termination releases the handle.", "Files.readAllLines / readString before parsing; an API whose only entry point returns a List; OutOfMemoryError on large imports; records with embedded newlines appearing as two malformed rows."),
      G("no_dialect_or_error_policy", "Hard-coding the comma, quote and UTF-8 assumptions and silently padding or dropping ragged rows, with errors that report no position.", "Model the Dialect as an immutable value object (delimiter, quote, escape mode, header, comments, trim, charset, BOM handling) and make ragged-row behaviour an explicit ErrorPolicy — strict throws with record number, line number and the raw row; lenient collects a rejects report; normalize pads only on opt-in.", "a parser constructor with nine boolean parameters or none at all; European semicolon files handled by a forked copy of the parser; an invisible BOM inside the first header name breaking column lookups; 'CSV parse error' with no line number on a million-row import."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.csv, "CSV parser class diagram"),
];

const xpFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 5 : l === "easy" ? 10 : l === "medium" ? 15 : 20);
const diffFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 0.1 : l === "easy" ? 0.2 : l === "medium" ? 0.4 : 0.6);
function pm(moduleId, o) {
  return { trackKey: TRACK_KEY, moduleId, topicId: o.topicId, exerciseId: o.exerciseId,
    position: o.position, level: o.level, type: "pattern_match", title: o.title, scenario: o.scenario,
    instructions: "Pick the best answer.", starterCode: "", expectedSolution: o.correct,
    blanks: [{ options: o.options }], testCases: [{ type: "pattern_match", correct: o.correct, explanation: o.explanation }],
    hints: [], xpReward: xpFor(o.level), difficulty: diffFor(o.level) };
}

